eir \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Deficit Sustained By Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Buying \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Because the overwhelming component in the current account deficit is the shortfall in the trade in goods and services - $150.3 billion in the second quarter - nations with trade surpluses with the U.S. accumulate massive stacks of dollars. They are used to buy U.S. Treasury and agency bonds, thereby enabling the U.S. to sustain its current account deficit, at least in the short run.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By supporting the dollar through these purchases, Asian countries also maintain the competitiveness of their own export industries. The depreciation of the dollar that would be necessary to help bring U.S. external payments into balance is delayed in the process.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Through buying a huge amount of these bonds, China and Japan are keeping inflation and interest rates low in the U.S., said Sung Won Sohn, chief economist at Wells Fargo Bank in Minneapolis. "Clearly, this cannot go on forever."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to his bank's calculations, the U.S. will need to find $2 billion a day from abroad to cover the deficit in 2005. He's also concerned that future generations of Americans will face a 10-hour work day, with four of those hours going towards payments on the interest on external U.S. liabilities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Long-Term Problem That Must Be Resolved \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No one knows exactly at what point the burden of financing the deficit will inflict serious pain on the dollar and the U.S. economy. Federal Reserve officials, from Chairman Alan Greenspan on down, have acknowledged it is indeed a long-term problem that must be resolved, but don't see much cause for worry in the short term.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An obvious danger