\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves level off, China excepted.  \b0\par\par\uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2004\par 05:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By John Mair\par     SINGAPORE, Sept 14 (Reuters) - Asia's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve holdings have levelled off over the last five\par months, with China the exception, and analysts say a stronger\par U.S. dollar has lessened the need for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Reserves held by Asian central banks reached $2.215\par trillion at the end of August, the latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  figures\par show. China is the only country not to have released reserve\par holdings for August.\par  \par     Asian reserves excluding China rose by $14.0 billion, just\par 0.6 percent, in August.\par     This was the largest monthly rise since March, when\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 reserves rose by $59.3 billion excluding China, and analysts  \f28 \par \par \uc2 said it largely reflected valuation effects and U.S. Treasury  \par \par \uc2 coupon payments in August.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Japan's reserves, for example, rose by $8.8 billion even\par though it did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hold down the yen during August.\par     Asian reserves excluding China have risen by just $15.6\par billion in the five months to the end of August, after rising\par by $210.7 billion in the first quarter of 2004.\par     In contrast, China's reserves rose by $43.2 billion in the\par four months that ended in July, a pace fairly consistent with\par the rise of $36.5 billion in the first quarter.\par     "They are the only big reserve accumulator through the\par region," Deutsche Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist James Malcolm said.\par     "It's just a reflection of real flows. People made a big\par deal of the current account being in deficit for one or two\par months. It's obviously not a major factor."\par     The floating Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were weaker against the\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar at the end of August than they were at the end of March.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth has slowed in the region and investors are looking  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 elsewhere after being large buyers of Asian equities in 2003  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 and into 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, the dollar has ben supported by expectations  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the start of an interest rate tightening cycle in the United  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 States.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The U.S. Federal Reserve raised rates on June 30 and August\par 10, and is widely expected to raise rates again next week.\par     "The point was, really, through the end of last year and\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the beginning of this year you had three very dominant forces  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in the market," Robert Rennie, Westpac Bank's chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 strategist, said of the slowdown in Asian reserves growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There was a strong acceleration in northeast Asia's trade  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 surplus, a dramatic pick-up in foreigners buying Asian equities  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 and a lot of speculation on potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These inflows put pressure on Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, authorities were worried that stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 would hit demand for exports, a key driver of growth in the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 region, and so central banks bought U.S. dollar assets, such as  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasuries, to cap the gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say the basically steady holdings since March show  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 that Asian central banks outside of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have not had to  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the same extent to stem \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This was because the environment had changed, Rennie said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade surpluses were still large, but not growing as quickly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further, foreigners had significant equity holdings and did not  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 need to buy as aggressively as before. The growth trade also  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 lost favour, and so the pressure on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to rise faded.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, a number of Asian central banks were thought to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 have been forced to buy their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in July and August  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to stem falls against the dollar that could have added to the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 inflationary pressures coming from rising oil prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think Asia is beginning to realise in a period of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 sustained strength in dollar commodity prices...maybe a bit of  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength is not a bad thing," Rennie said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve holdings:\par                Reserves       As at      end-2003   percent\par                   $ bln                     $ bln    change\par  Japan            828.0      Aug 31         673.5     +22.9\par  China*           483.0      Jul 31         403.3     +19.8\par  Taiwan           231.6      Aug 31         206.6     +12.1\par  South Korea      170.5      Aug 31         155.4      +9.7\par  Hong Kong        118.5      Aug 31         118.4      +0.1\par  India            117.5      Aug 27         100.6     +16.8\par  Singapore        101.3      Aug 31          96.3      +5.2\par  Malaysia          54.5      Aug 30          44.9     +21.4\par  Thailand          43.8      Aug 27          42.1      +4.0\par  Indonesia         34.8      Aug 31          36.3      -4.1\par  Philippines       16.0      Aug 31          16.8      -4.8\par  Pakistan          12.2      Aug 28          12.0      +1.7\par  Bangladesh         3.1      Aug 31           2.6     +19.2\par  Total          2,214.8                   1,908.8     +16.0\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *In December 2003, China used $45 billion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 reserves - about 10 percent of its holdings at that time - to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 bail out two banks. Those funds are not included in this  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 figure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: central banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040914e09e002jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
