\par\par \b \uc2 DJ IMF: China's Econ Slowing But 'Soft Landing' Not Assured  \b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2004\par 03:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--China's economy has slowed to a more sustainable pace of growth, but the risk of overheating remains, the International Monetary Fund said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 A move to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system would help the government diffuse the threat rapid economic growth might spark inflation, the IMF said in its annual 'Article IV' review of China's economy.  \par \par \uc2 'A crucial short-term concern is that despite the recent indications of moderation in the fast pace of investment and economic growth, a soft landing of the economy is not yet assured,' according to a press summary of the executive board's discussion.  \par \par \uc2 China's economy expanded at a 9.7% annual rate during the first half 2004, prompting the IMF to raise its forecast for full-year economic growth to 9.0%. This is up from the IMF's 8.5% prediction in its World Economic Outlook published in April.  \par \par \uc2 As the effects of tighter monetary policy continue into 2005, economic activity should maintain a more sustainable pace, the IMF said. IMF staff predict economic growth will slow to about 7.5% for 2005, down from the previous 8.0% forecast.  \par \par \uc2 Keeping the economy on track will require China to pursue 'prudent macroeconomic policies and accelerated structural reforms in key areas, including the banking sector, state enterprises, and labor markets,' the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 For years, the IMF has urged China to introduce more flexibility into its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system to make its easier for the economy to adjust to changing financial conditions. More recently, as the U.S. trade deficit with China exploded and U.S. manufacturers suffered recession, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the U.S. dollar has become a source of friction between the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the value of the yuan stable, despite a system of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. The large-scale dollar purchases have resulted in ever-growing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and an inflated domestic money supply.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF again urged China to adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, and suggested the current global financial climate presents a good opportunity to act.  \par \par \uc2 'Directors suggested that greater exchange rate flexibility is best introduced from a position of strength, as this would help limit any adverse effects on growth and employment,' the IMF said. 'Many directors therefore considered that, in view of the present favorable circumstances, it would be advantageous for China to make an initial move toward greater exchange rate flexibility without undue delay.'  \par \par \uc2 China should move cautiously in lifting its capital controls, the IMF said. A well-capitalized and sound banking system is a key requirement of capital account liberalization, but keeping capital controls shouldn't rule out greater exchange rate flexibility, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 Diversification of bank ownership and liberalization of loan rates would advance bank restructuring and lead to strong balance sheets, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF urged China's government to reduce budget deficits further next year, and introduce reforms to make Treasury management more transparent. For example, China should bring off-budget spending into the official budget and make local governments more accountable for spending.  \par \par \uc2 China should consider extending the Value Added Tax to services and change the VAT so it falls more heavily on consumption than production, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020040826e08q001ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
