\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ's Hirano says flexible yuan may come after 2006.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ritsuko Ando and Ritsuko Shimizu  \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2004\par 12:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 8 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan's point man for international issues said on Tuesday that China could shift towards a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy after it strengthens its financial system, which may happen by the end of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Executive Director Eiji Hirano also told Reuters in an interview that he did not expect China's economy to experience a drastic slowdown and that Japan and the rest of the world could withstand a moderate decline in its growth.  \par \par \uc2 He said that China's obligation to the World Trade Organisation to open up its finance sector in less than three years might mean it planned to strengthen its financial system by that time.  \par \par \uc2 "If it can strengthen its financial system, it can then accelerate the pace of relaxing capital controls and, parallel to that, consider a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," he said.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. manufacturers have led complaints that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which pegs the yuan to the dollar in a razor-thin band from 8.276 to 8.280, gives it an unfair trade advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese central bankers say that moving towards a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is a long-term goal but they have made it clear they are in no rush and will not bow to international pressure.  \par \par \uc2 Hirano said he believed that China's plans for a gradual implementation of a flexible yuan policy and financial reforms made sense, and that he was against putting international pressure on China for an early review of its yuan policy.  \par \par \uc2 "There should not be pressure on China. It is an issue that should be determined by China and it should not be a political tool," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Hirano also recommended, however, that China not fall behind in implementing change, citing Japan's post-war experience of drastic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, such as the yen's surge following the 1986 Plaza Accord.  \par \par \uc2 "I do think that the gradual approach is sensible, but it will be crucial to head towards flexible policy at an appropriate time so that imbalances don't build up," he said.  \par \par \uc2 While not part of the BOJ's nine-member policy board, Hirano is widely seen as one of the most influential officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 He is also one of the "sherpas" who have done the groundwork for Group of Seven statements in the past few meetings.  \par \par \uc2 A G7 statement last September called for flexible exchange rates, triggering a surge in the yen as traders interpreted it as a call on Japan to curb its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as on \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to loosen its grip on the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 RESILIENT GLOBAL ECONOMY  \par \par \uc2 On China's economy, Hirano said the chances of a "hard landing" were limited and that Japan and other global economies would probably weather a moderate slowdown.  \par \par \uc2 China, struggling to rein in an economy which grew 9.8 percent in the year through the first quarter, has taken a series of tightening measures, although it has so far resisted what would be the first rise in benchmark interest rates in nine years.  \par \par \uc2 "Despite its rapid growth, China's economy is not that large in terms of global GDP share. So even if there is a slowdown...one could say the impact on the global economy will not be that great," he said, while acknowledging some countries would be affected more than others.  \par \par \uc2 "Considering the strength of the global economic recovery, a slight slowdown, if it's a soft landing, can be withstood," he said, citing brighter U.S. and Japanese economic indicators.  \par \par \uc2 Hirano also said the world economy looked increasingly capable of weathering a widely expected interest rate rise in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 "Compared to a few months ago, I am more optimistic about the risks stemming from a U.S. rate hike or the start of an upward trend in global rates," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040608e068001j7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
