\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-China provides glimpse of future  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  regime.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2004\par 11:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, April 22 (Reuters) - In an ideal world, China should loosen the reins on its pegged yuan, let it move to a level markets consider fair and then let them determine its fate.  \par \par \uc2 But while the possibility of a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made headlines for more than a year, the investment community is struggling to work out when China will adjust its dollar peg system and what shape the new regime will take.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, markets got a glimpse of the future - and a sense China may move a little faster than previously thought. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, in a newspaper interview, said China planned to link the yuan to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it moved towards a flexible system. No timeframe was given.  \par \par \uc2 "It probably does accelerate the time horizon slightly, purely and simply because the implications are that studies of a basket-type system, similar to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's, are probably a bit more advanced than people were thinking," said Robert Rennie, a strategist at Westpac in Sydney.  \par \par \uc2 There are still big question marks about the nature of any basket system and economists differ on what they think would suit China and the international community. Analysts also wondered if China was ready - fundamentally, politically or technically.  \par \par \uc2 But markets at least now have something to chew on.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Guo Shuqing, told the Financial Times a floating system was favoured rather than a fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said Chinese authorities had done extensive research on the use of baskets. "In the past we had such a system. The floating system will also have some reference to a basket," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan is currently fixed in a razor-thin band of 8.268-8.280 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The comments, in line with what markets had guessed was the most likely direction, were the clearest indication to date of what China planned to do. There had been talk of a revalution at a stronger rate or a wider band against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 FEAR OF FLOATING  \par \par \uc2 Asia has never been comfortable leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the vagaries of markets and the 1997 Asian financial crisis is a powerful reminder of what havoc foreign capital can wreak.  \par \par \uc2 China and Malaysia have fixed their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar. Hong Kong's dollar peg is more than two decades old. Others actively manage their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Japan's yen is the closest in Asia to a free float, but even there the authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth volatility.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't move from one end of the spectrum to the other - a fixed peg to a free float - because that would reward speculators and encourage more betting. Forwards already price in a 4.5 percent rise in the yuan within a year.  \par \par \uc2 China could widen the yuan's 0.04 percent trading band, but that would still leave monetary policy in the world's sixth largest economy tied to the dollar's swings.  \par \par \uc2 In a basket arrangement, authorities decide on a neutral exchange rate index computed as the weighted average of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the basket, either nominal values or adjusted for price levels in the respective economies. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is then linked to that index rate.  \par \par \uc2 Economists reckon the Chinese yuan will be pegged to a basket of 10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which includes those of its biggest trading partners - the yen, dollar and euro - and some Asian units.  \par \par \uc2 Several firms think the yuan should first be revalued before moving to a "crawling basket peg", which would change or "crawl" regularly over time.  \par \par \uc2 "We are looking for a 10-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket around the middle of this year, initiated with a five percent decline in dollar/yuan," said James Malcolm, a strategist with JP Morgan in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE OR INDIA?  \par \par \uc2 Asia has two formal basket systems - India and Singapore - and analysts say China may emulate one of them.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore has what it calls a "nominal effective exchange rate band". It allows the Singapore dollar to move in an undisclosed range and acts as the economy's main monetary tool. Last week, the authorities said there were tightening policy by shifting that parity rate to allow for a stronger local unit.  \par \par \uc2 India's rupee has a monitoring band, which is similar. Among the most significant differences are that India's basket is adjusted for inflation while Singapore's is not, and India discloses the parity rate of its basket.  \par \par \uc2 Steve Gilmore, a strategist with Morgan Stanley in Hong Kong, argues a Singapore-type system might make it difficult for Chinese firms to hedge and be too opaque for international policymakers.  \par \par \uc2 "A more transparent and simple-to-administer basket with a limited number of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably makes more sense," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Signs from international policymakers are that change is on the way. Rhetoric from the United States and Europe - asking China to allow the yuan to strengthen - has moderated. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said this week it was a matter of time before China had a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "There is some recognition that the conditions for moving are becoming riper, said Malcolm.  \par \par \uc2 He noted speculative flows were diminishing. China's surplus was turning into a deficit. And there is less upward pressure on the yuan due to a global economic recovery and the prospect of higher U.S. interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Yet, as China is already seeking to rein in an overheating economy and prick a property bubble, it may be cautious about a yuan change. It could lead to a sharp reversal for the economy or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040422e04m00180\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
