\par\par \b \uc2 IMF: World Output To Grow 4.6% in 2004; 4.4% in 2005  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,037 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2004\par 17:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--The International Monetary Fund on Wednesday said the global economic recovery has gained momentum, and urged governments around the world to prepare for tighter fiscal and monetary policies.  \par \par \uc2 In its latest World Economic Outlook, the IMF raised its forecasts for world output growth to 4.6% in 2004 and 4.4% in 2005. For both years, the new forecasts are 0.6 percentage point higher than the IMF's estimates issued in September. The improvement reflects much stronger-than-expected performance by economies in the U.S., Japan, the U.K., Russia, and developing countries in Asia, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 "With global trade rising sharply, financial markets buoyant, and the U.S. economy rebounding, the balance of risks has significantly improved," the IMF said. "In the short run it is possible that global growth may be higher than projected, although geopolitical risks - including terrorist attacks - and oil prices have become increasing concerns."  \par \par \uc2 The IMF said forecasts were based on the assumption oil prices - a simple average of U.K. Brent, Dubai and West Texas Intermediate - would fall to an average of $30 per barrel for 2004, and $27/bbl in 2005. If oil prices were to be sustained at current higher levels, or rise even further, then growth would be adversely affected, IMF Research Director Raghuram Rajan told reporters in a briefing.  \par \par \uc2 But with recovery on an overall more secure footing, policymakers need to shift their focus from fostering economic growth to coping with higher interest rates, particularly U.S. rates, and reducing budget deficits and public debt.  \par \par \uc2 "If the recovery proceeds as expected, the time when the Federal Reserve will need to begin raising interest rates may be approaching, and it will need to continue to lay the groundwork for starting the move toward a more neutral monetary policy stance," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 For the U.S., the IMF predicted growth of 4.6% and 3.9% for 2004 and 2005. Underpinning the economy are strong business investment growth, and only a modest slowing in private consumption during 2004 as job growth and higher stock prices largely offset the fading support from tax cuts and home equity withdrawals. Structural changes in the U.S. economy probably delayed, not eliminated, the usual cyclical recovery in labor markets. With corporate profits at record levels, and unit labor costs down for two years, U.S. employment should pick up, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, the recovery in the euro area is more subdued and dependent on foreign demand - so much so that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should consider easing interest rates, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 "Growth is projected to increase to 1.75% in 2004 and 2.25% in 2005, but with recent economic indicators failing to improve, the robustness of the recovery is uncertain," the IMF said. "Given the benign outlook for inflation, the downside risks to growth argue for shifting to an easing bias in monetary policy."  \par \par \uc2 Economic activity is much stronger than in the euro area as a whole in Spain, Ireland, Greece, and the U.K., where to differing degrees the biggest threats to stability are corrections in housing markets. Even if the run-up in housing prices is justified by fundamentals, a correction could be triggered by higher interest rates, higher unemployment or slower growth in disposable income, the IMF said. Therefore, financial supervisors in countries experiencing housing booms should pay close attention in light of "relatively large mortgage exposures of banks, still-rising house prices, and the recent moves toward more speculative mortgage transactions," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF advised the Bank of England to stick with a policy of gradual tightening through small and steady interest rate hikes.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF raised forecasts for Japanese output higher, saying that the recovery there appears more sustainable.  \par \par \uc2 "We are seeing a rotation from export-led growth to investment and increasingly toward consumption," Rajan said. However, bank lending remains weak, underscoring the need for continued reforms in the financial system, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Throughout the industrial world, governments need to reduce budget deficits to prepare for the costs of an aging population. This is particularly urgent for the U.S., given its size and importance to the global economy, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. administration's commitment to halving the federal government deficit over the next five years is an important first step, although credible measures to achieve this reduction have yet to be put in place," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 Increasing U.S. savings through deficit reduction would also help lower the risk of an abrupt adjustment in the massive U.S. current account deficit leading to higher global interest rates, the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has depreciated by close to 20% since its 2002 peak, but the declines have been generally against other industrial countries, the IMF noted. Also, U.S. demand continues to outpace most of its trading partners, the fund said. "The U.S. current account deficit is projected to fall only modestly to 4% by 2009, suggesting that further U.S. dollar depreciation over the medium term may well be needed," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF strategy for reducing global trade and investment imbalances also includes familiar prescriptions for stepped-up structural reform and faster growth in the euro area, banking reforms in Japan, plus increased \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in the rest of Asia.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility would also benefit Asian countries individually, as well, the IMF said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is complicating monetary policy in Asia, particularly for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in that \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally increase liquidity in the banking system, Rajan said.  \par \par \uc2 "More flexibility would be in their own interests and increasingly you see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaking into the domestic credit system," he said. "If you get the price of the exchange rate right, and the exchange rate is part of the pricing system, you make better investment decisions."  \par \par \uc2 Still, China will have to clean up problem loans and weak financial institutions before it can lift capital controls and allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely, Rajan said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 21-04-04 1500GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040421e04l000rf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
