\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Fed's Poole-US rates must not stay low too long.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Alister Bull  \par \uc2 543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 17:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MEMPHIS, Tenn., March 25 (Reuters) - The U.S. economy is on track for solid growth and the Federal Reserve must ensure borrowing costs do not remain too low for too long, St. Louis Fed President William Poole said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "We do have to watch the data carefully and make sure policy does not remain accommodative beyond its time," Poole told reporters before speaking at events at LeMoyne-Owen College in Memphis.  \par \par \uc2 Poole, a voting member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee this year, said U.S. inflation was low although the risks of deflation had receded. He also said it was well understood that at some point the Fed would start raising rates from current 1958 lows of 1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "We all know it has to happen. It is only a question of timing and that depends on how vigorously the economy starts to expand," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed has said it can be patient in maintaining accommodative monetary policy and noted in its latest statement, issued after its regular policy meeting on March 16, that the employment situation was still lagging growth.  \par \par \uc2 But Poole said he expected hiring to improve substantially, although it was hard to say when, and voiced confidence in the balance and robustness of the U.S. recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "We're well placed for a long and solid business expansion...I think we've got lots of expansion ahead."  \par \par \uc2 OUTSOURCING  \par \par \uc2 He said the outsourcing of service jobs, which some have blamed for a so-called hollowing out of U.S. industry, was a painful but unavoidable process that adds up to a good thing.  \par \par \uc2 "I view what has been going on with service outsourcing as part of a long-term trend.  \par \par \uc2 "It is beneficial to the nation, it makes the nation wealthier, but it does produce significant adjustment costs for the individuals who lose their jobs," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Poole noted inflation was under control and that higher energy and other commodity costs were not yet an issue.  \par \par \uc2 "We have seen a transition over the year to a situation in which the risks of deflation have declined and the risks to the upside slightly outweigh the risks of the downside surprise.  \par \par \uc2 "But I think the consensus (inflation) forecast makes a lot of sense," he said, referring to market predictions that inflation will remain tame.  \par \par \uc2 He also noted that some of the gain in the dollar price of imported commodities reflected the dollar's weakness on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and that the picture in terms of euros was somewhat more benign.  \par \par \uc2 Some of the dollar's weakness has been pinned on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Asian governments to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak, particularly \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which operates a fixed peg between the yuan and the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. government has been campaigning to get China to let the yuan float. Poole was careful to avoid criticism of Beijing and expressed understanding of the difficulties it faces.  \par \par \uc2 "China has to worry about its overall monetary and financial stability and I think China needs to be careful in just setting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  free. It would be very difficult to manage," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040325e03p0037m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
