\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For March 16  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,918 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Bank of Japan's board voted unanimously Tuesday to hold monetary policy steady following strong economic data, a fall in the yen and stability in domestic markets, which together argued against a further boost to liquidity. Although the decision was in line with the expectations of most market participants, some analysts had speculated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could boost liquidity to maintain strong growth in Japan's money supply and reassure financial markets ahead of Japan's fiscal year-end on March 31. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained its usual statement that it will, if necessary, inject ample cash into the financial system regardless of its policy settings. ("=UPDATE: BOJ Leaves Policy Unchanged As Markets Steady" ran at 0531 GMT; 2nd UPDATE to include monthly economic report, Fukui and Tanigaki comments)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki Tuesday held firm his resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets despite a media report that Japan is looking for a way to end its massive yen-selling campaign. ("=UPDATE: Japan Tanigaki: No Change To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policy" ran at 0427 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =TAIWAN VOTE: Allegations that aides of Taiwan President Chen Shui-bian lied about political donations from a fugitive business tycoon have thrown the stock market into turmoil this week, and could help cost Chen his job. The affair underlines how closely politics and the financial markets remain linked in Taiwan, and how tense the political climate has become in the final days of campaigning before the island's presidential elections on March 20. ("=TAIWAN VOTE: Furor Over Donations May Boost Nationalists" ran at 0713 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Forget economic fundamentals. Japan isn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to save its exporters from a strong yen. It's really being strong-armed by a cunning White House that knows it needs Asian dollar-buying to plug the U.S. budget and current-account deficits and keep the Treasurys market from crashing. At least that's what ordinary Japanese are being told in a hugely popular comic. Facts aside, the popularity of current episodes of the assassin-thriller "manga" series "Golgo 13" shows how much \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has pervaded the mass consciousness here. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Catches Manga Assassin's Eye" ran at 0514 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CHINA\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Premier Wen Jiabao describes it as a critical juncture for China, as the government attempts to switch policy priorities from economic growth founded on investment toward boosting the individual spending power of farmers and workers. (=CHINA WATCH will run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Stronger-than-expected exports will likely help South Korea maintain a current account surplus for the 11th consecutive month in March. Indeed, the surplus in the first three months of 2004 is expected to reach the full-year target of $6 billion, according to the Bank of Korea's projections. ("=2nd UPDATE: S Korea Curr Acct Surplus To Top $5B Jan-Feb" ran at 0855 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Uncertainties stemming from an unprecedented impeachment against South Korean President Roh Moo-Hyun could delay economic recovery if the situation is prolonged, the Bank of Korea said in a statement Tuesday. ("Bank Of Korea: Prolonged Impeachment Crisis May Slow Econ" ran at 0231 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: China's broader banking sector will experience a surge in bad debts within years as much-needed risk management reforms fail to keep pace with a sharp increase in lending, Fitch Ratings said Tuesday. ("=INTERVIEW: More Chinese Banks Face Bad Debt Surge -Fitch" ran at 0617 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Indonesia's declining government debt bodes well for a possible upgrade in the country's sovereign rating outlook in the medium term, a Fitch Ratings analyst said Tuesday. ("=INTERVIEW:Indonesia Debt Fall May Help Rtg Outlook-Fitch" ran at 0548 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's housing bubble is deflating rapidly, with new home buyers locked out of the market while investors are beating a hasty retreat, possibly signaling an end to interest rate increases for now. ("=UPDATE: Australian Housing Investors Desert Mkt En Masse" ran at 0414 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Despite the government's attempts to encourage the development of the local bond market, the amount of Hong Kong dollar debt issued in 2003 declined, as a rebound in the stock market made other fund-raising options more attractive. ("HK Bond Issues Declined 3% In 2003 As Stock Market Rose" ran at 0339 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's fourth-quarter manufacturing sales rose 2.6% in real terms from the third quarter to NZ$14.8 billion, but economists said the strength of the headline number is deceptive, with growth in manufacturing actually modest. The manufacturing data are the last component forecasters need to finalize projections for New Zealand's economic growth in the fourth quarter, which will be released March 26. ("=UPDATE: NZ's 4Q Manufacturing Strength Deceptive" ran at 2251 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand retail bank ASB Bank continues to diversify its overseas funding, with its latest borrowing from the public market being an EUR80 million, two-year, floating-rate note. ("=NZ's ASB Bank Raises EUR80M Funding Through 2-Year Bond" ran at 0359 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar slipped against the yen in Asia Tuesday, weighed by continued speculation Japan may be examining backing away from its aggressive yen-selling campaign. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr/Yen Down; Questions About Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Stance" ran at 0544 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES:  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Conglomerate Wharf is expected to report later today higher full-year earnings, buoyed by a one-off gain from its distribution of shares in its pay-TV operator i-Cable Communications and port earnings. Core earnings will be hit, however, by lower office rents. (results expected after 0900 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1100)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese supermarket operator Daiei Inc.'s same-store sales for the year ended Feb. 29 were flat from the year earlier, beating the company's forecast of a 1% drop, a Daiei spokesman says. The news sent the company's share price sharply higher. ("Daiei Says Last FY Same-Store Sales Flat, Beating Target" ran at 0108 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Canon Inc. says it expects to post a record high group net profit for the January-March quarter, thanks to its strong performance in the first two months of the year. The major Japanese maker of cameras, printers and copiers has been enjoying solid earnings in recent years on brisk sales of multifunction copiers and digital cameras. ("Canon Likely To Post Record Group Net Pft For Jan-March" ran at 0618 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Don't lose heart if Japan's stock market rally fizzles later this year - an even bigger rally will begin in 2005, says a strategist at UFJ Partners Asset Management Co. ("=INTERVIEW: UFJ Partners Sees Powerful Stk Rally Late '05" ran at 0418 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Huaneng Power reports full-year earnings later today. The company is expected to post a 26% rise in 2003 net profit to CNY4.94 billion, fueled by a 27% increase in total generation during the year. (results expected after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN IPO FOCUS: The allure of a China play may draw investors to Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. when it debuts this week. But the stock may be overrated given its rich valuations and the risks posed by the cyclical, capital-hungry chip industry, say analysts. ("=ASIAN IPO FOCUS: The Real Test Begins Now For China SMIC" ran at 0834 GMT; see also "China SMIC Amends CFO's Comments On Capex, TSMC Lawsuit," which ran at 0110 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: As it passes the halfway mark of a five-year profit recovery program, Australian retailer Coles Myer has impressed investors and is a stock on the rise. However, smart money has been flowing into Australia's biggest retailer for the last 12 months and many investors say anyone preparing to buy in now should ask whether they have left that decision too late. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Australia's Coles Myer Getting Pricey" ran at 0318 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Epic Energy said Tuesday that its 28-member banking syndicate has agreed to defer a A$1.85 billion debt due Tuesday, as the U.S.-based company tries to finalize the sale of its Australian natural gas pipeline portfolio. ("Epic Energy Wins A$1.85 Bln Debt Extension" ran at 0757 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Hewlett-Packard plans to start selling Linux-based desktop personal computers in Asia, says a spokesman at its Japanese unit, in a move that will intensify competitive price pressure in the cutthroat PC industry. (story will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Haier Group is expected in the next few days to finalize a long-discussed plan to inject its white-goods manufacturing unit into Hong Kong-listed Haier-CCT Holdings, sources close to the matter said Tuesday. The Hong Kong Stock Exchange said in January it would treat backdoor listings as new listings from March 31, and Haier's backdoor listing seems planned to beat that deadline. ("China Haier Soon To Finalize HK Backdoor Listing -Sources" ran at 0515 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's Thanachart Bank, the country's smallest and newest bank, is moving full steam ahead to expand its business this year after getting permission from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to operate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and current account services earlier than scheduled. The bank plans to open 17 new branches this year, mostly in key provincial areas, in addition to its current seven branches, said its senior vice president Anuwat Luengtawekul. ("=INTERVIEW:Thai Thanachart Bk Set To Grow After Full Svc" ran at 0846 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: In a major step toward better protecting consumer rights in a rapidly expanding auto market, China issued its first automobile recall regulation, which will take effect Oct. 1. ("China's Automobile Recall Policy To Take Effect Oct 1" ran at 0752 GMT) [ 16-03-04 0911GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: State-owned Vietcombank will need two more years to improve its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  before it is ready to go public, said bank director Vu Viet Ngoan. ("Vietcombank Plans To Go Public In 2006 - Bk Official" ran at 0417 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwanese display maker Prime View International is looking into buying used liquid crystal display production equipment from a Japanese company as it expands capacity to cash in on rising demand, said a company executive. ("=INTERVIEW:Taiwan Prime View May Buy LCD Equip From Japan" ran at 0312 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Ministry of Construction and Transportation Tuesday said GM Daewoo Auto & Technology will voluntarily recall 163,977 units of its popular minivan model Rezzo due to an engine defect. ("GM Daewoo To Recall 163,977 Rezzos Due To Engine Defect" ran at 0115 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Sichuan Tianyi Science & Technology Co., a chemical and engineering company, said Tuesday regulators have impounded a small percentage of its stock held by troubled brokerage China Southern Securities. ("China Sichuan Tianyi: Stk Held By Southern Sec. Seized" ran at 0035 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: An international consortium, whose members include Royal/Dutch Shell Group and ExxonMobil, may soon decide whether to pull out from PetroChina's West-East gas pipeline project, China-based industry officials said Tuesday. ("Intl Cos May Pull Out From PetroChina Gas Proj Amid Talks" ran at 0309 GMT) [ 16-03-04 0911GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040316e03g000co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
