\par\par \b \uc2 US Sends Delegation To China To Discuss  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 , Bk Issues  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Deborah Lagomarsino  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2004\par 21:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--A six-member U.S. delegation left for China Monday to meet with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government officials on technical issues related to laying the groundwork for China's shift to a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Treasury said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The working group, which U.S. and Chinese officials agreed to form late last year, will meet with Chinese officials Wednesday and Thursday, Treasury said. It is the first in series of meetings the group is expected to hold.  \par \par \uc2 The group is led by Treasury and headed up by David Loevinger, deputy assistant Treasury secretary for Africa, Middle East and Asia.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration has been pressing China to adopt a flexible exchange rate. Treasury Secretary John Snow traveled to China last year to press the administration's case on the issue and President George W. Bush discussed it in December with China's Premier Wen Jiabao during his U.S. visit.  \par \par \uc2 China has indicated that it is committed to shifting to a floating exchange rate over the long term, but has not given a timeline for the shift.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightly manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls, effectively pegging it at 8.3 yuan per U.S. dollar. U.S. manufacturers want China to revalue to a stronger rate, arguing the yuan is undervalued, giving Chinese producers an unfair competitive advantage.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. delegation will meet with officials from the People's Bank of China, China's banking sector and the government, Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 The working group's aim is "technical cooperation" on a wide range of financial issues relating to an eventual shift in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. These include regulatory matters, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives, the banking sector, Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Deborah Lagomarsino, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9255; deborah.lagomarsino@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040223e02n0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
