\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China weighs  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  reform as debate rages.   \b0\par\par\uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2004\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser comment, SAFE statement, economist comment)   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Feb 12 (Reuters) - A Chinese state newspaper said on Thursday allowing the yuan to rise would help ease trade friction, fuelling the protracted debate over the outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid official pledges to make it more flexible in time.   \par \par \uc2 Senior officials have repeatedly said there is no plan to revalue the yuan, but that the exchange rate policy would be perfected in stages.   \par \par \uc2 "The outside should stop speculating," an adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who declined to be identified, told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2 "It's very clear now: the first step will probably be a widening of the current band, then a move to a basket system, but it is not a revaluation. I don't know any time-table."   \par \par \uc2 The China Securities Journal said in a front-page commentary that allowing a rise in the yuan, pegged to the dollar at around 8.28 since the mid-1990s, would help ease friction with trade partners caused by its relative weakness as the dollar declines.   \par \par \uc2 "Helped by a change in trade-related tax policy, the renminbi appreciating by five percent against the dollar would help solve the problem of the worsening trade conditions for China," the newspaper said, citing an estimate by economists.   \par \par \uc2 Trade friction showed the negative impact of the yuan's fall was growing, despite the economic benefits, it said.   \par \par \uc2 The United States, pressing for a revaluation of the yuan which it says gives Chinese goods an unfair advantage, has curbed textiles, televisions and other imports from China.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the main drive for China to reform the yuan regime was concern about economic overheating. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to buy hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to strong foreign investment and a traditionally big trade surplus.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hampered in its efforts to control the macro-economy," said Gao Huiqing, economist at the government think-tank State Information Centre.   \par \par \uc2 "RATIONAL AND BALANCED LEVEL"   \par \par \uc2 Premier Wen Jiabao pledged on Tuesday to keep the yuan exchange rate stable and perfect the exchange rate system, a line used many times by senior Chinese officials over recent months, and he reiterated that the country had no plans to revalue.   \par \par \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) Governor Zhou Xiaochuan said in a statement published by Xinhua news agency on Wednesday the yuan would "remain basically stable at a rational and balanced level" and China would perfect the exchange rate system.   \par \par \uc2 In reporting the Xinhua story, Japan's Nihon Keizai Shimbun on Thursday said Xinhua had quoted Zhou as saying China's exchange rate mechanism would be revised this year.   \par \par \uc2 But economists said the Xinhua report was not so specific.   \par \par \uc2 "I read the statement by the PBOC governor and I really don't see anything different from what he said before," said Hong Kong-based Citigroup economist Yiping Huang.   \par \par \uc2 "There might be a mild revaluation later in the year but there will unlikely be anything in the first quarter or next month," Huang said.   \par \par \uc2 "Actually, since there's been so much talk, the probability has been reduced," Huang said. "They don't really want the speculators to make huge profits. So when the market is moving I think they are unlikely to make a move."   \par \par \uc2 The premium on Chinese yuan non-deliverable forwards for the one-year tenor touched 4,450 points early on Thursday before pulling back to around 4,000, which implies an expected 5.1 percent appreciation in the yuan in a year's time.   \par \par \uc2 China wants to reform the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime at a time of its own choosing and may move to prevent a sharp appreciation.   \par \par \uc2 The State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) reiterated on Thursday it would spur capital outflows by encouraging firms to invest overseas and allow them to retain more hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would help ease upward pressure on the yuan.   \par \par \uc2 It would control short-term foreign debt and capital inflows and reform the interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, it said in a statement on its Web site:   {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.safe.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.safe.gov.cn}}}\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said China may be quietly weighing its options, with a re-pegging to a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help create flexibility and deflect U.S. criticism gaining in favour.   \par \par \uc2 China's trade balance dipped into deficit for the first time in 10 months in January as a tax rebate cut slowed exports.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said on Wednesday the value of the yuan would rise if \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  ceased \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040212e02c001e6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
