\par\par \b \uc2 China forwards price in higher yuan appreciation.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 09:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Feb 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets raised their expectations of an appreciation of the Chinese yuan on Monday after the Group of Seven's call for flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a Chinese newspaper report suggesting the yuan would be revalued next month.   \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  denied the report in the China Business Post that the tightly controlled yuan or renminbi, might be allowed to rise next month and its trading range would be expanded to five percent on either side by the end of 2005.   \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, markets clearly expect China will have to relax its grip sometime this year, perhaps more because of domestic compulsions than due to any international criticism that it is keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially weak.   \par \par \uc2  On Monday morning, there was renewed selling of the U.S. dollar in the yuan's offshore non-deliverable forward (NDF) market. One-year NDFs were quoted as far as 4,500 points, the highest yuan premium in three weeks, pricing in a 5.7 percent appreciation in the yuan in a year's time.   \par \par \uc2  The yuan is effectively pegged around 8.28 per U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2  Michael Jansen, markets strategist with the National Australia Bank in Sydney, said markets were merely riding the momentum around renewed talk of a yuan revaluation.   \par \par \uc2  "I think the major motivation is that everyone's coming around to the view that there will be some change in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime and 12 months is a good part of the curve to put these positions on," Jansen said.   \par \par \uc2  Peter Redward, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Deutsche Bank, agreed. Markets certainly weren't buying China's rebuttal of the report in the semi-official newspaper, he said.   \par \par \uc2  "The market has tended to discount the report. Having said that, it certainly shows sentiment towards revaluation and I wouldn't completely dismiss the view," Redward said.   \par \par \uc2  "NOT BECAUSE OF G7"   \par \par \uc2  The Group of Seven's veiled criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's and Asia's undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in September last year had little effect on the region's policymakers.   \par \par \uc2  And analysts expected this time to be no different.   \par \par \uc2  The group's communique on Saturday calling for more flexibility in exchange rates for countries that lack such flexibility, would not get China to move its yuan. After all, China is not even a part of the G7.   \par \par \uc2  China said on Monday it had been examining exchange rate reforms for a long time, but not because of any G7 pressure.   \par \par \uc2  "The G7 communique certainly does not have the teeth for China to move," said Bhanu Baweja, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with UBS.   \par \par \uc2  China has resisted pressure to revalue the yuan for more than a year, even when NDFs moved to record premiums in October and when the United States imposed quotas on Chinese textiles.   \par \par \uc2  But it will have to move, analysts said, because of the risks of an overheating domestic economy, inflationary pressures and the difficulties of managing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that have crossed $403 billion.   \par \par \uc2  "It will move when the noise is slightly less, the speculation is slightly less and all cyclical indicators are positive," Baweja said.   \par \par \uc2  "We think it can happen sometime this year but we think the market is massively overpricing the extent to which China is going to move."   \par \par \uc2  EXPENSIVE TRADES   \par \par \uc2  Making money by holding a long yuan position through NDFs was becoming more of a challenge as the trades got more crowded and China stuck to its rigid exchange rate regime, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2  "If China does not move and you sell dollar/yuan, you lose five percent," said Deutsche's Redward, referring to the discount the dollar now trades at against one-year yuan.   \par \par \uc2  Having paid that steep five percent premium to buy the yuan for a forward tenor, speculators are losing money the longer China stays pat, he explained.   \par \par \uc2  The yuan would have to move at least 10 percent higher before trades in NDFs yielded returns, he said.   \par \par \uc2  An alternative is to buy a basket of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Indian rupee, Singapore dollar, Korean won and Taiwan dollar which are more liquid, appreciating slowly and sure to move higher if China's yuan did.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040209e0290016k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
