\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 5-G7 agrees  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  volatility a risk to growth.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Glenn Somerville  \par \uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2004\par 03:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  BOCA RATON, Fla., Feb 7 (Reuters) - Finance chiefs from rich nations agreed on Saturday a global recovery was gaining steam and papered over tensions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates by warning markets "excess volatility" was not to be tolerated.   \par \par \uc2  As finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven wrapped up a two-day meeting at a plush Florida resort, they sought to calm fretful \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and to offer a balm to Europeans worried their rising euro could hurt growth.   \par \par \uc2  "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth," the G7 said in a closing communique. "We continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate."   \par \par \uc2  The statement by the United States, Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy and Japan was a calculated effort to counter the impact of their announcement in Dubai last September urging "more flexibility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which prompted a dollar sell-off that took its value down 10 percent against the euro.   \par \par \uc2  But analysts said while the statement may give pause to markets in the short term, it will not change a longer term downward trend for the dollar stemming in part from record U.S. budget deficits.   \par \par \uc2  "I think we are much closer to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Marcel Kasumovich, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Merrill Lynch in New York, referring to the possibility that a continued appreciation in the euro's value could lead Europe to counter it by selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buying dollars.   \par \par \uc2  LET MARKETS REIGN   \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, at a press conference afterward, made clear that was not something the free market-oriented Bush administration wanted to see and repeated a familiar refrain that competition should reign.   \par \par \uc2  "A strong dollar is in our national interest," Snow said. "But the relative values of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are best established in open competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Nobody can devalue their way to prosperity."   \par \par \uc2  The G7 statement went a step further, saying there should be "more flexibility in exchange rates ... for major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility," in an apparent reference to Asian countries that peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to others - as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  links its yuan to the dollar - or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to affect values.   \par \par \uc2  Snow turned aside a question on whether China, which the United States wants to loosen its 8.3 yuan peg to the dollar, was the intended target. He said only that the statement was agreed by the full G7 and said "it speaks for itself."   \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanagaki denied that Japan, which has spent heavily to keep its yen from rising in value and sapping its exports, was one of the countries that lacked flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues.   \par \par \uc2  "That's not the case," he said after the G7 meeting, indicating that Tokyo would keep up its market forays when the yen got out of line with fundamentals, a resolve which could be tested as early as next week.   \par \par \uc2  In fact, the United States felt getting more specific references to the need for flexibility into the communique was worth giving Europe a concession by inveighing against volatility. Nor did the resulting compromise lessen pressure on China - by some measures now the world's second largest economy - to speed up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform.   \par \par \uc2  MANY MANIPULATORS   \par \par \uc2  China is not the only country that manipulates its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . South Korea and Taiwan, among others, are regularly criticized for doing so. Some G7 participants made lightly veiled references to the Asian countries.   \par \par \uc2  "The various \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are not flexible will recognize themselves," European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said. "There is not only one, there are quite a few."   \par \par \uc2  European officials arrived at the meeting, held in a luxurious resort on Florida's wealthy "Gold Coast" near Miami, determined to have the Dubai wording altered to clarify that it was not intended to sanction a soaring euro that could imperil their export-led recovery.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. view after the meeting was that Europe had been wrong in feeling the Dubai statement sanctioned a dollar sell-off but that it was worth accommodating them as long as the importance of flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was preserved.   \par \par \uc2  The G7 participants, while allowing that a global economic recovery was quickening, warned the pace was uneven and said countries needed to redouble their efforts to boost growth.   \par \par \uc2  The officials also wagged a finger at Argentina, urging it in the statement to "engage constructively" with creditors and live up to its pledges to multilateral lenders.   \par \par \uc2  A number of G7 officials have expressed frustration at Argentina's slow progress in talks with private bondholders aimed at restructuring $88 billion in defaulted debt.   \par \par \uc2  Buenos Aires has held fast to its offer, made in Dubai last year, of 25 cents on every dollar of nominal debt.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040208e0280002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
