\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Feb 6  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,041 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 10:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data released Friday shed more light on Tokyo's unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign to stop a sharp rise in the yen that could hurt exports. ("=WRAP: More Details On Japan's Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0421 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The head of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's state asset-management body said the government is in no hurry to sell down state equity in listed companies, but analysts said Friday it looked like he was preparing the way for another round of state-owned share sales. ("=WRAP:China Offcl Seen Prepping Mkt For State Share Sales" ran at 0822 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan will tell its Group of Seven counterparts it will take action against excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday, underscoring Tokyo's unwavering resolve to keep the yen from surging as the dollar slides. ("Tanigaki -2: Underscores Commitment To Capping Yen Rise" ran at 0023 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The Bank of Korea decided Friday to keep the overnight call rate target unchanged, maintaining the rate at a record low of 3.75% in the face of weak domestic consumption. ("Bk Of Korea/Call Rate -2: No Significant Change Until 2H" ran at 0242 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: Growing demand for energy to fire China's fast-paced economic expansion has prompted authorities to tighten controls on exports of the country's vast coal reserves. ("=CHINA WATCH: Coal Energizing China's Move To Market Econ" ran at 0407 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Expectations are growing that U.S. data Friday could show solid job growth, which may offer modest support for the dollar just as G7 finance chiefs gather for a high-profile meeting. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Big Ben, Bush TV Spot Suggest Data To Aid Dlr" ran at 0406 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: South Korea's Woori Bank cut the size of its first global bond offering this year to between $300 million and $400 million Friday - from the originally targeted $500 million - and is expected to price the issue at a cheaper-than-expected level, after a recent sell-off in emerging market debt damped investor appetite. ("=WRAP: S Korea Woori Bk Cuts USD Bond Size On Weak Market" ran at 0804 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Moody's Investors Service Friday signaled it may soon upgrade Malaysia's sovereign rating, spurring buying in blue-chip stocks. The upgrade of the outlook on Malaysia's Baa1 rating to positive from stable comes just over three months after Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi assumed office. ("=WRAP: Moody's Hikes Malaysia's Rtgs Outlook, Aiding Stks" ran at 0730 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: If maiden bonds were launched like new ocean liners, there would be a lot of bubbly splashing around the Singapore dollar market. Depfa ACS Bank, the Irish subsidiary of the U.K.'s Depfa Bank and an affiliate of Germany's DePfa Deutsche Pfandbriefbank, is the latest to join the party with a S$150 million 2007 bond, its first in the Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=ASIA DEBT: Depfa Joins Growing Singapore-Dlr-Bond Party" ran at 0640 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Experience versus youth. This was one of the battlegrounds staked out this week as Australian Prime Minister John Howard and major opposition Labor leader Mark Latham embarked on a dummy election campaign. ("=INTERVIEW: Age Debate Puts Australian PM On Defensive" ran at 0142 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Retail sales growth in Hong Kong accelerated again in December, official statistics issued Friday show, underlining the recent recovery in local consumer spending. ("DATA SNAP: HK Retail Sales Growth Accelerates In Dec" ran at 0827 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Dai-Ichi Mutual Life Insurance, Japan's second-largest life insurer, has bought foreign bonds unhedged against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in the second half of the fiscal year and cut its holdings of domestic stocks and government bonds. ("=INTERVIEW: Dai-Ichi Life Ups Unhedged Foreign Bond Hldgs" ran at 0746 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: U.S. securities firm Bear Stearns Cos. returned Friday to Japan's Samurai bond market for the first time since 2001, pricing Y27 billion of five-year bonds. ("=WRAP: Bear Stearns Revisits Samurai Mkt, Sells Y27B 5-Yr" ran at 0753 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA CORP BONDS: A surge in Kangaroo bond deals took Australian corporate bond market issuance to A$2.5 billion in January, marking the most active start to a year on record. ("=Australia Corp Bonds: Kangaroos Drive Record Jan Supply" ran at 0301 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: After an early jump, the dollar stayed in a tight band against the yen in Asia Friday, as traders took to the sidelines ahead of U.S. jobs data and the Group of Seven finance officials' meeting in Florida, which starts later in the global day. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Treads Water Ahead Of G7, US Jobs Data" ran at 0604 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES:  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Matsushita Electric Industrial Friday reported strong profit growth for the October-December period, on brisk sales of its "Panasonic" audiovisual products led by DVD/hard-disc drive recorders and flat-screen televisions. The results beat the Japanese consumer electronics giant's own estimate, and the company lifted its full-year profit forecasts, underscoring its gains from the fast-growing digital products market. (WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's cash-strapped government plans to sell its stake in West Japan Railway while the domestic stock market appears solid, but traders fear the move could hurt the market's rally. ("Govt's JR West Shr Sales Plan Fuels Worry On Mkt Outlook" ran at 0352 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan Airlines System reported massive losses for the April-December period, as the spread of SARS and the conflict in Iraq drove its revenue from international flights sharply lower. ("Japan Airlines Sys -2: Slump In International Flight Rev" ran at 0629 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Nationalized Ashikaga Bank will release a new management plan later Friday, Japanese Bank Minister Heizo Takenaka said. ("Takenaka/Ashikaga -2: Hopes New Plan To Boost Earnings" ran at 0038 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Woori Bank said Friday its net profit nearly doubled last year to KRW1.33 trillion from KRW779.6 billion in 2002 due to reduced loan-loss provisions, improved asset quality and higher interest income. ("=WRAP: Woori Bk FY Net Up;Lower Provisions,Improved Assets" ran at 0446 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: SK Telecom, South Korea's largest mobile operator, said Friday its fourth-quarter net profit nearly tripled to KRW433 billion from KRW162.6 billion a year earlier, with an increase in subscribers and wireless Internet usage boosting its results. ("=WRAP:SK Telecom 4Q Net Surges On Subscriber Growth, Data" ran at 0854 GMT; second WRAP with more details will run after 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's LG Electronics remained in the red in the fourth quarter due to a charge related to its struggling LG Philips Displays joint venture. The diversified electronics company, which makes home appliances and mobile handsets, posted a net loss of KRW17.2 billion for the three months ended Dec. 31, narrower than the year-ago loss of KRW179.1 billion. ("S Korea LG Elec/Results -3: JV Restructure Hurts 4Q" ran at 0516 GMT; WRAP will run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shares of property developer Shanghai Forte Land debuted on the Hong Kong main board Friday, ending 7% higher than their IPO price of HK$3.25 on strong retail investor interest. But the shares didn't do as well as other recent listings due to market weakness of late. (WRAP will run around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Many of Japan's major trading houses reported strong earnings results for the April-December period as business reform measures taken in recent years have finally started to bear fruit, and as the stronger stock market trimmed their securities appraisal losses. ("=WRAP: Japan Trading Houses Report Solid Apr-Dec Results" ran at 0754 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: John Stewart, the new chief executive of National Australia Bank, said Friday that he will consider making senior management changes, but wants an investigation into rogue foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options trading to be completed first. ("Australia's NAB/CEO -2: Mgmt Should Have Monitored Risk" ran at 2140 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China Minsheng Banking Friday said a former manager of one of its branches in Beijing was sentenced to a 20-year jail term for extending 18 million yuan in illegal loans and appropriating another CNY51.6 million. ("China Minsheng Bk Manager Sentenced To 20 Yrs For Graft" ran at 0117 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan memory-chip maker Winbond Electronics said Friday it swung to a net profit of NT$211 million in the fourth quarter, from a loss of NT$1.92 billion a year earlier, amid a steady recovery in the semiconductor sector. Winbond also said it plans to start building a US$1.3 billion 12-inch wafer plant in the third quarter this year, and is still in talks with potential partners about a joint investment for the project. ("Taiwan/Winbond/Results -3: 4Q Gross Margin 22% Vs 20% 3Q" ran at 0713 GMT; "Taiwan Winbond/Plant -2: Talks With Potential Partners" ran at 0606 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: The joint venture between TCL International and France's Thomson SA propels the Hong Kong-listed consumer electronics company to big-league status, making it the world's biggest television set manufacturer. While analysts laud TCL's foresight in moving beyond a saturated home market, they are also concerned about where the company is headed in the long term, given the uncertain outcome of the TV venture and the likely sale of its lucrative mobile handset business. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Analysts Cautious About TCL's TV Foray" ran at 0100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The new head of investment banking in Australia for ING Bank NV is looking to snare more merger and acquisition work as the bank beefs up its investment banking activities. ("=INTERVIEW: ING Australia Invest Bk Head Sets M&A Agenda" ran at 2120 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Hong Kong and Malaysian stocks are expected to outperform their regional peers in 2004, helped in part by their relative underperformance in recent years and their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' peg to the weak U.S. dollar, Standard & Poor's Investment Services said Friday. S&P also likes South Korea because it is a "large, self-sustained, well-diversified economy that will benefit from global growth," its Director and General Manager for Asian Equities Charles Wheeler said Friday. ("S&P Expects HK, Malaysia,S Korea Stks To Outperform In 04" ran at 0903 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Hefty provisions for poorly performing property assets in Singapore could drag CapitaLand back into the red for 2003, despite the company's strong performance in the China and Australasian markets. Southeast Asia's largest listed property developer releases full-year results this afternoon. (results expected around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Yamaha Motor said its earnings in the April-December period were sluggish as the strong yen hurt its sales in North America. ("Yamaha Motor -2: Strong Yen Hurts Sales In North America" ran at 0258 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Midland Realty has doubled its stake in Chun Wo Holdings to 10.13% as the real estate agent continued to increase its exposure in the construction company in the open market. ("HK Midland Realty Buys 10% Stake In Chun Wo For HK$73.7M" ran at 0014 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Asian crude market players are assuming that OPEC won't cut March crude supplies even before the group meets Feb. 10. ("=OUTLOOK: Asia Crude Mkt Assumes No OPEC March Output Cut" ran at 0902 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Canada-based aluminum producer Alcan Inc. expects a strong market for alumina to continue well into 2004 given the robust fundamentals underpinning the market, Richard Yank, president of the company's Pacific bauxite and alumina \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Friday. ("INTERVIEW:Alcan Eyes Strong Alumina Mkt;Firm Fundamentals" ran at 0142 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040206e026000bo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
