\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Europe presses for market-soothing G7 statement.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Love  \par \uc2 988 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 21:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOCA RATON, Fla, Feb 6 (Reuters) - European finance chiefs will urge Group of Seven counterparts meeting in a Florida resort this weekend to craft a clear statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the U.S. dollar's dive from accelerating and crippling euro zone exporters.  \par \par \uc2 With the United States showing no concern about the pain the dollar's dive could cause the euro zone, European ministers said they also want to press the United States to help by cutting its record budget deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking in Washington en route to the G7 gathering, French Finance Minister Francis Mer said the group's last message on sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues needed clarification.  \par \par \uc2 Mer told reporters \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had not understood the full message in the statement that followed September's G7 meeting in Dubai urging large economies to adopt more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.  \par \par \uc2 "We will try to do the maximum together to state that the wording of Dubai does not satisfy anyone and try to hatch a wording or expression that satisfies all of us," Mer said.  \par \par \uc2 A G7 government source said European delegates believed a further euro surge would "constitute a problem." But he said dollar weakness was being driven by huge U.S. budget and trade deficits and Washington needed to tackle these.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. says that to reduce the U.S. imbalance there must be more growth in the rest of the world ... We say yes, but this in itself won't reduce the U.S. imbalance," the source said.  \par \par \uc2 Asked by reporters in Boca Raton whether the United States recognized Europe's worries, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow replied: "We are looking forward to a good meeting here."  \par \par \uc2 Snow was speaking at the start of the first of a series of bilateral meetings with G7 counterparts, this one with British Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPE HAS A PROBLEM  \par \par \uc2 European governments have fretted for months that the euro is rising too steeply against the dollar, which skidded to record lows against the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month and risks stifling the region's export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 European officials have repeatedly argued that the Dubai call for flexibility was aimed chiefly at Asian countries - particularly \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which use \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak and their exporters competitive.  \par \par \uc2 "It's clear we didn't succeed in Dubai in the form of expression we used ... In Boca Raton, our presentation will have to be different to what it was in Dubai if for no other reason than to show markets that our position was badly expressed or to flesh it out according to (recent) trends," Mer said.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 source said too few \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were bearing the burden of the dollar's fall since Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 "We wanted to widen the field of flexibility," said the G7 source, adding that while the euro and sterling rose after Dubai, the Bank of Japan continued to put a brake on the yen and the Chinese yuan remained steady.  \par \par \uc2 "The problem we have is how to meet the Dubai objective given that with China the policy of public declarations does not work. Private conversations work," said the source.  \par \par \uc2 SNOW PLAYS HOST  \par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary Snow is hosting fellow finance chiefs from Japan, Germany, France, Britain, Italy, Canada and top officials from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , European Commission, the euro group of finance ministers and the International Monetary Fund at a plush nearly 80-year-old Florida resort.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 meeting is the first of four set for this year. The exclusive economic club has been famed for periodic agreements over the past 20 years to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  collectively to halt extreme movements in the world's major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 However, markets are wagering against a repeat performance. While the dollar stabilized in January after European officials publicly protested the speed of its fall, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets remain on edge about how G7 will deal with further losses, and the greenback fell again on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 While few if any analysts expect any dollar accord this weekend, many have speculated about a shift in wording in the G7 statement that would warn of the consequences of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.  \par \par \uc2 The United States has been sanguine about a weaker dollar, which helps its exporters and economy in an election year, and has had no adverse effects on inflation rates.  \par \par \uc2 Snow earlier this week offered up his oft-repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mantra, telling a congressional panel the Bush administration supports a strong dollar - but carefully adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values are best set in competitive markets.  \par \par \uc2 Those markets have punished the dollar in recent months. Over the past two years, it has fallen some 29 percent against the euro and 20 percent against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japan comes to the table seeking a tacit endorsement of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen reined in and said beforehand it will tell G7 partners it plans to continue.  \par \par \uc2 Japan Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki seemed resolute at a news conference in Tokyo on Friday. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should move stably, reflecting economic fundamentals. If there are moves that deviate from that, we will take appropriate action," he said. Tokyo spent 5.8755 trillion yen ($55.55 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the October-December quarter.  \par \par \uc2 But the absence of booming China, by some World Bank measures now the world's second-largest economy, means any message to Asian countries about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy may pass unheeded.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have said they want strategies to boost global growth atop the agenda. Other items include the financing of emerging economies, progress on tracking terror-related funds and the reconstruction of Iraq.  \par \par \uc2 Argentina may face some pressure to be more flexible in its slow-moving debt renegotiation talks with private bondholders.  \par \par \uc2 "We haven't ruled out inviting Argentina in the statement to show good faith to its creditors," one the G7 source said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040206e026002hz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
