\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Bank of East Asia, Hang Seng cut HK savings rates.   \b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2004\par 08:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Jan 10 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's Bank of East Asia Ltd (0023.HK) and Hang Seng Bank Ltd (0011.HK) announced on Saturday lower local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit rates amid a flood of liquidity in the city's banking system.   \par \par \uc2 The banks said separately they would cut their savings rates for Hong Kong dollar deposits to 0.001 percent from 0.01 percent, with effect from Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Their moves tracked that of HSBC Holdings Plc (0005.HK) (HSBA.L) announced on Friday, and may be followed by other Hong Kong lenders.   \par \par \uc2 Bank of East Asia, the territory's fifth-largest lender, said in a statement the new rate would apply to accounts with balances of HK$5,000 (US$641) or more, while accounts with less than that amount will get no interest, as before.   \par \par \uc2 Hang Seng Bank, the local unit of HSBC, said the new rate would apply to deposits of HK$10,000 or more, while those below that amount will receive no interest.   \par \par \uc2 Shares in Bank of East Asia closed at HK$24.70 on Friday, having gained about 14 percent in the past three months. Hang Seng shares ended at HK$102.50, and had added more than six percent during the same period.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's banking system is awash with liquidity as foreign investors pile into the city's stock market and park cash there for investments in booming mainland \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money markets in recent months, selling Hong Kong dollars in a bid to curb growing spot market strength in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is pegged to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) has sold HK$11.45 billion this week alone, bringing the aggregate balance - a key measure of interbank liquidity - to nearly HK$44 billion. (US$1=HK$7.8).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040110e01a0043b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
