\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of E. Asia cuts HK savings rate amid liquidity flood.   \b0\par\par\uc2 278 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2004\par 04:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Jan 10 (Reuters) - The Bank of East Asia Ltd (0023.HK), Hong Kong's fifth-largest lender, announced on Saturday a cut in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit rates amid a flood of liquidity in the city's banking system.   \par \par \uc2 The bank said in a statement it would cut its savings rate for Hong Kong dollar deposits to 0.001 percent from 0.01 percent, with effect from Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The move tracked that of HSBC Holdings Plc (0005.HK) (HSBA.L) announced on Friday, and other Hong Kong lenders may follow.   \par \par \uc2 Bank of East Asia said the new rate would apply to accounts with balances of HK$5,000 (US$641) or more, while accounts with less than that amount would get no interest, as before.   \par \par \uc2 The best lending rate will remain unchanged at five percent, the bank said.   \par \par \uc2 Shares in Bank of East Asia closed at HK$24.70 on Friday, and had gained about 14 percent in the past three months.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's banking system is awash with liquidity as foreign investors pile into the city's stock market and park cash for investments in booming mainland \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money markets in recent months, selling Hong Kong dollars in a bid to curb growing spot market strength in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is pegged to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) has so far sold HK$11.45 billion this week alone, bringing the aggregate balance - a key measure of interbank liquidity - to nearly HK$44 billion. (US$1=HK$7.8).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040110e01a0028l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
