\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-HSBC cuts HK savings rate amid liquidity flood.  \b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 14:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Jan 9 (Reuters) - HSBC Holdings Plc (0005.HK) (HSBA.L) on Friday announced a steep cut in Hong Kong dollar deposits rates amid a flood of liquidity, a move that may spark similar cuts by other banks and stoked fears of negative rates.  \par \par \uc2 HSBC's local arm, the Hongkong and Shanghai Banking Corp Ltd (Hongkong Bank), said in a statement it would cut its savings rate in Hong Kong for local dollar deposits to 0.001 percent from 0.01 percent, effective on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 It did not rule out negative interest rates for local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits in the future.  \par \par \uc2 The new rate will apply to accounts with balances of HK$10,000 (US$1,282) or more. Depositors with less than that will get nothing, as before.  \par \par \uc2 However, Hongkong Bank said it would not change its best lending rate, currently 5.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 HSBC shares added 0.8 percent to end at HK$126.50 in Hong Kong on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 No official at the bank was immediately available for comment but analysts said the savings rate cut was prompted by the ample liquidity in the banking system.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it (the unexpected HSBC cut) mainly reflects high liquidity," said Tony Liu, an analyst at DBS Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "The HIBOR rate has been very weak in the past quarter," he said, referring to the Hong Kong interbank offered rate.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's banking system is awash with money as foreign investors pile into its surging stock market and park cash for investments in booming mainland \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money markets in recent months, selling Hong Kong dollars in a bid to curb growing spot market strength in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is pegged to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) has so far sold HK$11.45 billion this week alone, bringing the aggregate balance - a key measure of interbank liquidity - to nearly HK$44 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "When a bank cuts its interest rates, other banks may follow suit," Peter Wong, chairman of the Hong Kong Association of Banks, told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 However, Liu said other banks may not wish to follow suit to keep customers. He noted HSBC traditionally had the lowest savings rates of Hong Kong banks given its dominant market position.  \par \par \uc2 In view of the large balances held in banks' clearing accounts with the HKMA, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has warned that it might impose a charge on large balances "in extreme or exceptional circumstances".  \par \par \uc2 "If the HKMA imposes a punitive charge, we will consider passing it onto customers," Raymond Or, general manager of Hongkong Bank, told reporters. Transferring that charge to customers could lead to negative savings rates.  \par \par \uc2 However, the HKMA said later on Friday it had no current plan to impose such charges on banks.  \par \par \uc2 "The HKMA currently does not envisage the need to introduce such deposit charges," an HKMA spokesman told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 (US$=HK$7.8)  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Katie Hunt and Brian Kelleher).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e019002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
