\par\par \b \uc2 Greenspan Says Stronger China Yuan Won't Impact U.S. Jobs  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Deborah Lagomarsino  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 866 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2003\par 02:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan on Thursday disputed the argument that a rise in the value of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would do much to help the U.S. job market, saying it would have little, if any, impact.  \par \par \uc2 "A rise in the value of the renminbi would be unlikely to have much, if any, effect on aggregate employment in the United States," Mr. Greenspan said in prepared remarks to the World Affairs Council of Greater Dallas.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightly manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls, effectively pegging it at 8.3 yuan per U.S. dollar. U.S manufacturers want China to revalue to a stronger rate, arguing the yuan is undervalued, giving Chinese producers an unfair competitive advantage.  \par \par \uc2 "The renminbi is widely believed to be markedly undervalued, and it is claimed that a rise in the renminbi will slow exports from China to the United States, which according to some, will create increased job opportunities for Americans at home," Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 "The story on trade and jobs, in my judgment, is a bit more complex, especially with respect to China, than this strain of conventional wisdom would lead one to believe," Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration has urged China to adopt a flexible exchange rate. In meetings with Bush this week, China's Premier Wen Jiabao reiterated China's commitment to shift to a floating exchange rate over the long term.  \par \par \uc2 If China's yuan were to appreciate, U.S. imports from China would presumably fall as China loses competitive position to other low-wage economies, Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 "But would, for example, reduced imports of textiles from China induce increased output in American factories? Far more likely is that our imports from other low-wage countries would replace Chinese textiles," Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 Despite China's large trade surplus with the U.S., overall Chinese trade is "much closer to balance," he said.  \par \par \uc2 As for China's management of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, Mr. Greenspan noted that the country's current practice could threaten to overheat the economy down the road.  \par \par \uc2 China currently buys large amounts of U.S. Treasurys to maintain its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. It has been offsetting, or sterilizing, these purchases by reducing loans to commercial banks, selling bonds and raising reserve requirements.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Greenspan pointed out that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commercial bank reserves have been rising enough to support money-supply growth well in excess of a 20% annual rate so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 "Should this pattern continue, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be confronted with the choice of an overheated economy, with its potential recessionary consequences, or a curtailing of dollar asset purchases. The latter presumably would allow the renminbi to appreciate against the dollar," Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration also has been pushing for China to ease capital controls. Mr. Greenspan said it isn't clear what impact China's changing its capital controls would have on the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "No one truly knows whether easing or ending of capital controls would ease pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, in the process, also eliminate inflows from speculation on a revaluation," Mr. Greenspan said  \par \par \uc2 "Many in China, however, fear that an immediate ending of controls could induce capital outflows large enough to destabilize the nation's fragile banking system," he said.  \par \par \uc2 As for the current upward pressure on the yuan, Mr. Greenspan said it isn't clear how much of that results from underlying market forces, capital inflows from speculation China will revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or from capital controls that suppress Chinese residents' demand for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 A "misaligned" Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have adverse effects on the global financial market and indirectly on U.S. output and jobs, Mr. Greenspan cautioned.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Greenspan also challenged another strain of conventional wisdom that has attributed the weak U.S. labor market to the widening trade deficit, low-priced competition from abroad and increasing foreign outsourcing by U.S. firms.  \par \par \uc2 "In fact, as Council of Economic Advisers Chairman Greg Mankiw recently pointed out, U.S. 'job losses are...more closely related to declines in domestic investment and weak exports than to import competition,"' Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 "In addition, of course, increased productivity has enabled ongoing demand to be met with fewer workers," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Greenspan again cautioned about the risks to the U.S. economy from any move to protectionism. In a speech several weeks ago, Mr. Greenspan said there are some signs protectionism may be emerging.  \par \par \uc2 "For the most part, we as a nation have not engaged in significant and widespread protectionism or more than five decades," Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 "The consequences of moving in that direction in today's far more globalized financial world could be unexpectedly destabilizing," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Such a development would likely lead to lower incomes, profits and jobs, Mr. Greenspan said.  \par \par \uc2 "So, yes, we can shut out part of all foreign competition, but we would pay a price for doing so -- perhaps a rather large price," he said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Deborah Lagomarsino, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9255; deborah.lagomarsino@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031212dzcc00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
