\par\par \b \uc2 China Ctrl Bk Official: Fast Loan Growth To Fuel NPL Rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2003\par 11:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--Bank loans in China have been growing too rapidly on an uptick in the economy, which will lead to a rise in nonperforming loans, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 If the fast pace of loan growth continues unchecked for several years, it could also put pressure on the yuan, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 "Our loan growth has been too fast since August last year...as the economy improved," said Dai Genyou, director of the Bureau of Credit Administration of the People's Bank of China.  \par \par \uc2 Yuan-denominated loans extended by commercial banks in China reached 2.53 trillion yuan ($1=CNY8.28) in the first 10 months of this year, Dai said.  \par \par \uc2 This compares with CNY2.25 trillion in the same period in 2002.  \par \par \uc2 Historically, an improved economy results in an increase in loans due to greater investment in various projects. But the continued investment is likely to result in supply exceeding demand after some time, and when the loans come due, "this will obviously result in a rapid growth in nonperforming loans," Dai said.  \par \par \uc2 If the excessive pace of lending continues unchecked for several years, the accumulative inflationary effect could be sufficient to place pressure on the yuan to depreciate, said Dai.  \par \par \uc2 The official said the pace of lending in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have been greater without \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The PBOC has asked Chinese commercial banks to keep lending in the second half of this year around the levels of the same period last year, Dai said.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the renminbi, is only fully convertible on the trade account. Even then, it is kept in a tight band of around CNY8.277 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 -By Lena Lee with Cui Aiping, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610-6588-5848; lena.lee@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031205dzc5000fr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
