\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Ends Higher As Exporters Sell US Dollars  \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2003\par 10:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan ended higher against the U.S. dollar Wednesday, unaffected by U.S. President George W. Bush's remarks that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Japan should stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, as exporters sold their excess dollar holdings.  \par \par \uc2 The discount in the one-year nondeliverable forward market for the yuan narrowed for the second straight session.  \par \par \uc2 In an interview with Asian journalists Tuesday ahead of his arrival in the region, Bush said "markets ought to be determining respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar ended at CNY8.2766 Wednesday, compared with its Tuesday close of CNY8.2767. The dollar traded between CNY8.2765 and CNY8.2770 during the session.  \par \par \uc2 "Market participants just shrugged off Bush's comments, as China's government is unlikely to react to such remarks," said a trader at a foreign bank in Shanghai.  \par \par \uc2 Late Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced that China would lower the tax rebates it pays to exporters by an average three percentage points, in a move seen as an attempt to allay concerns about China's exports being unfairly priced because of the yuan's de facto peg to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A cut in the rebates, to take effect in January, essentially raises the cost of Chinese goods.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar sales from exporters dominated the market Wednesday, the trader added.  \par \par \uc2 In the offshore market, the bid-offer quotes for the one-year USD/CNY NDF were at a discount of 3550-3250 to the yuan late Wednesday, narrowing from 3950-3850 on Tuesday afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 The NDFs reflect market psychology about the exchange rate but have no direct influence on the actual dollar-yuan rate.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known as the yuan or renminbi, is freely convertible mostly just on the current account, and even then the rate is kept within a tight range near 8.28 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar ended up at CNY1.0685 from CNY1.0678. The Japanese yen rose to CNY7.5918 per 100 yen from CNY7.5382, while the euro advanced to CNY9.7021 from CNY9.6135.  \par \par \uc2 -With Zheng Jin, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218 3268; djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 1153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031015dzaf000i8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
