\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:Asia Still Market Force Despite Bush Pressure  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,103 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 20:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever and Grainne McCarthy \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--For those who thought Asian central banks would quietly fade into the background following the Group of Seven's call last month for less \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, it may be time for a rethink.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Recent developments suggest they've no intention of allowing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at the mercy of dollar-bearish market forces, to appreciate against the greenback and imperil their export-led economic recoveries.  \par \par \uc2 The latest U.S. Federal Reserve data show foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of U.S. government securities surged to a massive weekly average of $18.2 billion for the week ended Oct. 8, compared with the already hefty $10.2 billion the week before. Analysts say much of this was by Asian central banks. And on Tuesday, several of them were reportedly selling their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, while Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unveiled a new batch of measures to curb speculative trading on the baht.  \par \par \uc2 Bluntly put, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out the appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar will continue. And this message of defiance appears to be aimed principally at China, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan is virtually pegged to the depreciating dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The bottom line is that the basic policy stance of Japan, of leaning into yen strength in an environment of dollar weakness, hasn't changed," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York. As evidenced by their actions, that also applies to other central banks in the region, he said.  \par \par \uc2 All this comes as the political uproar about Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies grows ever louder in the U.S., heading into a presidential election year. On Tuesday alone, President George W. Bush said he'll tell the leaders of both China and Japan that they need to let market forces determine the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Speaking ahead of a trip to Asia later this week, he also reaffirmed the strong dollar policy. Bush will visit Tokyo for talks with Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi before attending a meeting of leaders from the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum in Thailand.  \par \par \uc2 Asked specifically how he views Japan's efforts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the yen artificially weak against the dollar, Bush said, "the markets ought to be determining respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Bush made the comments during an interview with correspondents from Asian newspapers at the White House.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        Political Pressure Mounts \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, a group of senior democrats on Capitol Hill kept the pressure on the administration, with a letter urging Bush to be more aggressive in combatting what they call \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation by China and Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are concerned that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Japan and other countries are \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to keep the values of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  low," according to a letter to Bush signed by Sens. Thomas Daschle, D-S.D.; Max Baucus, D-Mont.; Charles Schumer, D-N.Y.; Debbie Stabenow, D-Mich., and Paul Sarbanes, D-Md., the ranking minority member of the Senate Banking Committee.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Specifically, they recommended the Treasury Department, in a regular \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report due this month, formally declare that China, Japan and other countries are manipulating their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to gain unfair trade advantage. This would trigger formal negotiations between the U.S. and these countries to end the manipulation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In another twist, Federal Reserve Governor Ben Bernanke took the other side of the debate, cautioning lawmakers not to press China to immediately scrap its exchange-rate system, saying that could perversely hurt U.S. exporters by causing the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lose value.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I do think that a purely floating exchange rate would provide dangers in the current financial system in China," Bernanke said. "One risk...is that there would be such significant capital outflows as Chinese citizens tried to make investments abroad that the yuan might actually depreciate, which would worsen the competitive situation," for U.S. exporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against this backdrop, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market was a volatile place on Tuesday, yet again, with the dollar hitting its weakest level of the day - around Y108.67 - against the yen in the wake of Bush's remarks. And there's every reason to believe this choppiness will continue.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's still some vulnerability you have to be careful of," said Rebecca Patterson, global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York. "Following Bush's comments, the market's again on watch for strong-arming tactics by the U.S. at the Koizumi meeting and APEC summit."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par       It's Not Over Yet \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But, there's little evidence Asian central banks are about to halt their efforts to support the dollar completely. In Asian trading Tuesday, the monetary authorities of South Korea, Taiwan, Hong Kong and Japan - who along with China control vast portions of global dollar reserves - reportedly sold their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for dollars. The effect in some cases was dramatic, with the South Korean won, for example, plunging nearly 2% briefly down to levels last seen before the G7 meeting in Dubai called for greater flexibility in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rocketed also against the Thai baht after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula announced measures to limit the use of accounts held by nonresidents with local financial institutions for short-term speculative purposes. "The baht has strengthened too much and we don't want it to strengthen further," Devakula told reporters Tuesday in blunt terms.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So both covert and overt methods of trying to prevent local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation are being employed. "They're using all the cards in their deck to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from over-inflating," said Thomas Molloy, a trader at Bank Leumi in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That doesn't necessarily mean Asia's governments are trying to turn around a moving train of dollar weakness. But they are trying to slow it down, something they're likely to continue to do, regardless of U.S. political pressure. "In general, Asia's into smoothing mode," said Patterson.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London. Grainne McCarthy is Assistant Managing Editor for fixed income, macroeconomics and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news. Grainne was previously Indonesia bureau chief and also reported for Dow Jones Newswires in Belgium).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Alex Keto, Elizabeth Price and Joseph Rebello in Washington contributed to this story)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031014dzae0002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
