\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 13  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,086 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2003\par 11:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: When it comes to Japan, it doesn't take much for China's masses to fly into a fit of frenzied indignation. So the state media easily worked themselves into a lather over a sex-laden tale of Japanese businessmen cavorting with an orgy of prostitutes at a ritzy hotel in the southern Chinese city of Zhuhai, near Macau. The reaction in China again highlights that despite a continuing improvement in the investment and trade climate between the two East Asian rivals, postwar enmities continue to linger. ("=CHINA WATCH: War Enmity Fails To Halt Closer Japan Links" ran at 0329 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: International credit ratings agency Fitch Ratings Monday revised its outlook for China's long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating to positive from stable, matching similar moves by rival agencies, Moody's Investor Services and Standard & Poor's Corp. The announcement presages the launch Monday in Hong Kong at a roadshow of a new sovereign bond expected to be made up of a 10-year, $1 billion tranche and a five- or seven-year EUR500 million tranche. ("=WRAP: Fitch Revises China Rating Outlook To Positive" ran at 0430 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy-makers face a tough week, torn between a desire not to upset the U.S. by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen and fears that if they step aside, the resulting rise in the local unit could harm the economy. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Yen To Drift Up Ahead Of Bush's Tokyo Visit Fri" ran at 0414 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's banking industry regulator Monday published draft rules on over-the-counter financial derivatives business conducted by commercial banks and other financial institutions. ("China Issues Draft Rules On OTC Financial Derivatives" first take ran at 0805 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The People's Bank of China said Monday it will offer 10 billion yuan in short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills this week, in a cautious resumption of credit tightening following a holiday earlier in the month. ("China PBOC To Issue CNY10B Bills In This Week's \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0902 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: Another Golden Week holiday has come and gone, with nearly 300,000 mainland tourists visiting Hong Kong and spending by some estimates as much as 1.5 billion yuan. That splurge is a boon to Hong Kong retailers, but a headache for the People's Bank of China, the mainland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=CHINA WATCH: Yuan Notes Pour Into HK, But Then Where?" ran at 0228 GMT; see also "=WRAP: China Visitors Reviving Hong Kong Tourism Industry" that ran at 0510 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said Monday he favored a slightly weaker Thai baht, noting the unit's recent rise could have "some effect" on the country's exports. Pridiyathorn's comments - at the sidelines of the World Economic Forum's East Asian summit in Singapore - seemed at odds with last month's statement by the G-7 countries, which sought more flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. ("=INTERVIEW: BOT Governor" first take ran at 0157 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean President Roh Moo-hyun on Monday proposed holding a national referendum in December to determine whether he has the public's trust and said he would step down if he fares poorly. ("WRAP:S Korea Pres Proposes Natl Referendum To Gauge Trust" ran at 0650 GMT; second WRAP with implications for country's economic outlook will run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: A leadership crisis surrounding South Korean President Roh Moo-Hyun won't impact the country's sovereign rating for now but it is expected to delay the economic recovery, said a sovereign analyst for Fitch Ratings Monday, adding that the agency is a "little" disappointed with the political leadership in South Korea. ("=INTERVIEW: Fitch" first take ran at 0439 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's President Roh Moo-Hyun Monday said the government has the responsibility to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to safeguard the stability of financial markets, hinting at continued selling of the Korean won to slow its rise against the U.S. dollar. ("S Korea Pres: Govt Should \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Protect Markets" ran at 0425 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  must move swiftly to prepare for a predicted explosion in inbound and domestic tourism, a World Travel and Tourism Council report released Monday said. (story will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =RBI WATCH: India will likely calibrate its monetary and economic policies to accommodate an import-led widening of its current account deficit in the coming quarters, heralding a welcome rebound in domestic investment and consumption. ("=RBI WATCH: Policy To Accomodate India Curr Acct Gap" ran at 0505 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KARACHI: Pakistan's government is set to launch a "second generation" of economic reforms, focusing on boosting the pension funds industry, expanding the corporate bond market and further measures to deepen the country's capital markets, said Finance Minister Shaukat Aziz. ("=INTERVIEW: Pakistan To Launch New Econ Reforms - Aziz" ran at 0721 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Vietnam's State Treasury Monday sold 300 billion dong worth of five-year dong-denominated bonds to five local institutions at an annual yield of 8.5%, a treasury official said. The sale is part of the Treasury's plan to sell a total VND3 trillion of five-year bonds by the end of 2003. ("Vietnam Sells VND300B Dong Bonds; VND300B More Next Wk" ran at 0520 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar was mixed against its major counterparts during Asian hours in holiday-thinned trading. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0711 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: A decision by U.K. drinks giant Allied Domecq to walk away from the battle for Peter Lehmann Wines has simplified the situation for shareholders, said Richard England, chairman of the upmarket Australian wine producer. ("=INTERVIEW: Allied Exit Offers Clarity To Lehmann Holders" ran at 0147 GMT; WRAP will run around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Nanya Technology, the world's fifth-largest maker of computer memory chips, Monday posted its third straight quarterly net loss for the July-September period, citing higher costs for testing its products. Nanya recorded a third-quarter net loss of NT$169 million, reversing from a NT$590 million profit in the year-ago period. But the company said it expects fourth-quarter shipments to rise 16% from the third quarter, ahead of the busy year-end holiday season. ("Taiwan Nanya Tech/Results" first take ran at 0719 GMT' "Taiwan Nanya Tech/4Q Shipments" ran at 0726 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Ford Motor's chairman and CEO Bill Ford said Monday the U.S. auto maker and its Japanese affiliate Mazda Motor will invest a combined 21 billion baht in their Thai joint venture, Auto Alliance (Thailand) Ltd. ("Ford/Mazda/Thai JV" first take ran at 0750 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Chinese non-life insurer PICC Property & Casualty Co.'s IPO is expected to raise as much as HK$5.3 billion, a person close to the deal said Monday, which will make it the biggest public share offer in Hong Kong this year. Priced between HK$1.3 and HK$1.7 a share in a 3.1 billion share offer, the deal will enable the company to raise HK$4 billion to HK$5.3 billion. ("REPEAT:China PICC P&C/IPO-2: Likely Start Trading Nov 6" ran at 0148 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: General Motors affiliate, Daewoo Auto & Technology, said Monday it has signed a preliminary agreement to acquire Daewoo Powertrain for $44 million from Daewoo Telecom. Daewoo Powertrain produces automatic transmissions for passenger cars and is 100% owned by Daewoo Telecom. ("GM Daewoo/Powertrain" ran at 0154 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Analysts are becoming increasingly confident that there are no more skeletons in AMP Ltd.'s closet, clearing the way for a possible takeover bid from National Australia Bank or a rival suitor. ("=WRAP: Analysts More Optimistic About Australia's AMP" ran at 0605 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =Asia DRAM Report: DRAM traders and analysts are becoming jittery over the lack of pre-holiday demand buildup for personal computers, which may indicate plenty of inventory left for firms and the likelihood that DRAM spot prices will continue their slow decline. (=Asia DRAM Report will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's largest minicar maker, AviChina Industry & Technology Co., is expected to raise up to HK$1.93 billion from a share offer priced between 95 HK cents and HK$1.21 a share, a person familiar with the deal said. ("AviChina/IPO" first take ran at 0206 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: U.S. venture capital firm Crystal Ventures LLP has begun approaching potential investors for its new fund, seeking up to $150 million to invest in Asian and U.S. technology companies. ("=INTERVIEW: Crystal Ventures Seeks $150M For Tech Fund" ran at 0340 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Indonesia's PT Bank Panin will need to find a foreign partner or pull out of its bid for a controlling stake in PT Bank Internasional Indonesia, the head of the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency said Monday. Last month, IBRA shortlisted three consortia to compete for a 51% stake in BII, Indonesia's sixth-largest bank. ("=INTERVIEW: IBRA Demands Foreign Bank Bids For BII, Lippo" ran at 0518 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Woori Finance Holdings said it revised up unit Woori Bank's first-half net profit by 198.3 billion won as the bank underestimated some profit from sale of nonperforming loans. ("S Korea Woori Bank/Revises" first take ran at 2359 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: State-owned Krung Thai Bank expects its operating profit in the third quarter to remain steady on year and on quarter, a senior bank official said Monday. ("=INTERVIEW:Krung Thai Bank Expects Steady 3Q Oper Profit" ran at 0835 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Optical frame maker Moulin International Holdings said it will buy German framemaker NiGuRa Optik GmbH for EUR10 million from Rodenstock Group. ("HK Moulin Intl Buys German Framemaker NiGuRa For EUR10M" ran at 0340 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong-listed TCC International Holdings estimates it could earn HK$5.4 million from the disposal of its 9.87% share in Taiwanese mobile phone service provider KG Telecommunications. ("HK TCC Intl Hldgs Details Gain From KG Telecom Share Sale" ran at 0135 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Airways' strong presence in the region and relatively low labor costs bode well for the flag carrier's plan to set up a low-cost airline, but alliances with other budget airlines in the region may be needed to ensure long-term success, an airline industry expert said. ("=INTERVIEW: Planned Thai Budget Airline May Need Tie-Ups" ran at 0833 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Harvey Norman Holdings' Finance Director John Skippen said Monday that second quarter sales are very strong after the Australian retailer posted a 22.1% jump in first-quarter sales to A$867.5 million. ("=INTERVIEW: Harvey Norman" first take ran at 0241 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's Guthrie Property Development Holding Sdn. Bhd. says its profit margins may be lower than the industry average but that's because it spends more to build residential housing superior to the competition's. Guthrie Property plans to list on the Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange in a few months time, and will assume the listing status of sister company Highlands & Lowlands Bhd., in a restructuring that was announced in May. ("=INTERVIEW: Malaysia's Guthrie Ppty Defends Lower Margins" ran at 0844 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China's September crude oil production fell to a six-month low of 13.85 million metric tons, down 3.8% from August, reflecting bottlenecks in crude production in the country's northeastern fields, a China-based industry official said Monday. ("China Crude Oil" first take ran at 0658 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Newcrest Mining, Australia's largest gold miner, said it produced 192,738 ounces of gold in the first quarter ended Sept. 30 at a cash cost of A$182 an ounce. That compares to 198,992 ounces at a cash cost of A$181 an ounce in the fourth quarter ended June 30. The company turned a net profit of A$17.9 million in the quarter. ("Australia's Newcrest/Output" first take ran at 0208 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031013dzad000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
