\par\par \b \uc2 Overshoot of Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  seen as threat to recovery.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ovais Subhani  \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 08:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 2 (Reuters) - Asian central banks are buying billions of dollars to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising, but growing speculation they will lose this battle threatens an overshoot of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength that may hurt regional economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said a call by the Group of Seven for more flexible exchange rates - interpreted by markets as criticism of Asia's export-protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has strengthened the perception that central banks will struggle to contain speculative inflows.  \par \par \uc2 This "hot" money, together with portfolio investment and rising trade surpluses, has pushed the Japanese yen up four percent since the mid-September G7 meeting and sent the Thai baht and South Korean won to three-year highs.  \par \par \uc2 "The perception of a major change in \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy carries significant dangers," said Frank Gong, co-head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Bank of America in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 Asia's relatively high interest rates, rallying stock markets and signs of strong recovery in the second half of 2003 are proving attractive lures to long-term investors worried about a weakening U.S. dollar and low rates.  \par \par \uc2 These factors are also attracting the attention of speculators, such as hedge funds, who make money from short-term investments.  \par \par \uc2 But the region still bears the scars of the 1997/98 financial crisis, and remains wary of "hot" money which can leave as quickly as it comes.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said $15 billion of hot money had flowed into the island so far this year and added that this threatened financial market stability.  \par \par \uc2 By deducting the rise in reserves from the basic balance of payment surplus - current account surplus plus foreign direct investment - Gong estimated that about $24 billion of hot money has already flown into China during the first half of the year.  \par \par \uc2 On the same measure, hot flows for South Korea stood at $7.7 billion for the first seven months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a lot of hedge fund money going around," said Hong Kong-based Morgan Stanley economist, Andy Xie.  \par \par \uc2 "These people are getting excited, interpreting this (G7 call) as some massive historical shift, yen going to 80, renminbi appreciating by 20 percent," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Gong and Xie believe a weak Asian banking system means strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hurt exports and company profits rather than translating into higher domestic demand.  \par \par \uc2 Normally a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps strengthen domestic demand by boosting purchasing power, but Asia's large overhang of non-performing loans means banks may remain unwilling to lend.  \par \par \uc2 Gong said the Nikkei average's five percent fall since the G7 call for greater flexibility showed investors were worried that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength may sap growth.  \par \par \uc2 Xie said deflation in Japan, China, Taiwan and Singapore was still a major policy concern, and it could be exacerbated by excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.  \par \par \uc2 ONE-WAY BET  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were expected to strengthen this year as the region's export-driven economies were perceived as major beneficiaries of a forecast recovery in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 But the pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise has intensified as the United States, worried by its jobless recovery and huge budget and trade deficits, has turned up the heat on the region, particularly \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to stop the dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has inflated the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to near $1.7 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 Asian authorities have long argued that they would allow appreciation based on cyclical strength, but would not permit their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to become a one-way bet for speculators.  \par \par \uc2 Marios Maratheftis, an economist at Standard Chartered, said the global realignment of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and correction of imbalances was supposed to be a long-term process and central banks in Asia should ensure that any adjustment was gradual and orderly.  \par \par \uc2 "Yes, I would expect them (speculative flows) to increase. But I would expect authorities to be in there to control them," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Eddie Wong, chief strategist at ABN Amro in Hong Kong, said the equity rally in most of Asia ex-Japan had been driven by foreign inflows - but noted the exposure of major global funds to Asia was still far from what it was before the Asian crisis.  \par \par \uc2 "The flood of global liquidity into Asia has only just started," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031002dza2001oj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
