\par\par \b \uc2 Latin America Stands To Benefit If Asia Budges On  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Esterl  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,004 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2003\par 16:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--U.S. and European governments are jawboning their counterparts in Asia to revalue their cheap \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which could provide a welcome lift to another region that's quietly watching the debate from the sidelines.  \par \par \uc2 Latin America has long lagged its peers in developing Asia when it comes to export competitiveness, a trend that hasn't been helped by policy decisions in places like Beijing and Seoul aimed at keeping local exchange rates from appreciating too quickly.  \par \par \uc2 That competitive advantage eventually could narrow, though, if Asian authorities heed Saturday's call by the Group of Seven industrialized nations to allow greater flexibility in exchange rates - a practice already introduced by many Latin American governments in recent years.  \par \par \uc2 "If we were in a scenario in which (Asian countries) allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float, that might help Latin America a little bit, especially Mexico," said Carlos Janada, a Latin American economist at the IDEAglobal consultancy.  \par \par \uc2 While China has kept its yuan fixed against the dollar since 1994 at an exchange rate that is now considered undervalued, Mexico's peso was floated the same year and, after an initial plunge, has strengthened considerably in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  period. Recently, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  surpassed Mexico as the No. 2 exporter to the U.S., behind Canada, much to the consternation of manufacturers south of the Rio Grande.  \par \par \uc2 Marc Chandler, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC, said several of his bank's clients have indicated "they'd be happy to" take bigger positions in Mexico if it becomes clear China will agree to loosen its reins on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and allow the yuan to strengthen.  \par \par \uc2 Stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia also could give a boost to other countries in Latin America, which has bet increasingly on export-fueled growth with mixed results since beginning to open up its economies in the 1990s after several decades of protectionist policies.  \par \par \uc2 Ecuador, El Salvador and Panama would automatically benefit. The three countries have fully dollarized their economies, so their goods immediately would become cheaper on world markets if any Asian countries agree to strengthen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  vis-a-vis the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 But Latin American countries with free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also could use a hand as they try to expand a still-shaky export base. After a decade of free-market reforms, Latin America and the Caribbean's percentage of exports to gross domestic product barely budged, to 16.5% in 1999 from 14.1% in 1990, according to World Bank data. By contrast, the percentage in East Asia and the Pacific rose to 38.1% from 26% over the same period.  \par \par \uc2 It's far from clear if Asian governments will budge on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. China until now has balked at growing international pressure to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , insisting Tuesday that the issue is an "internal affair."  \par \par \uc2 Guillermo Ortiz, Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, told reporters Tuesday that Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation "isn't a scenario that is likely" and that Mexico should try to ensure greater competitiveness by making its own economy "more flexible."  \par \par \uc2 Economists agree that countries like Mexico haven't helped their own cause by faltering on long-promised reforms such as overhauling the tax system, increasing private sector investments in the energy sector and easing labor laws.  \par \par \uc2 "It's hard to compete with China on a number of fronts, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just one of those problems," said Rafael de la Fuente, a Latin America economist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 While no countries in Latin America boast the size of China's workforce and few can compete with its extremely low labor costs, they also haven't been able to keep pace with other parts of emerging Asia where better-trained workers churn out lucrative technology exports for overseas markets.  \par \par \uc2 Although countries such as Costa Rica were able to draw significant investments from U.S. computer chip giant Intel in the 1990s, the world's three largest chip foundries are located in Taiwan and Singapore. Latin America's export base, by contrast, still skews predominantly toward commodities and other materials farther down the value-added chain.  \par \par \uc2 Still, a more evenly balanced playing field when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't hurt if recent history is any indication.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil is expected to post a record trade surplus of more than $20 billion this year, prodded along in large part by last year's 35% \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation. South America's biggest country had rung up an uninterrupted string of trade deficits between 1995 and 2000, when its exporters were held back by an overvalued fixed exchange rate designed to slay inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Neighboring Argentina also has watched its export sector steadily recover since scrapping the peso's decade-old fixed parity with the dollar in January 2002. With the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now worth only a third what it was before the devaluation, Argentine exports have jumped 17% in the first seven months of 2003 from the year-earlier period as the country starts to recover from a four-year recession.  \par \par \uc2 With long-standing inflation pressures ebbing in many corners of Latin America, few central banks have been compelled in recent months to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from further depreciation and some - such as Argentina - have even moved to ensure that they don't start to strengthen again.  \par \par \uc2 A bit of extra help from across the Pacific wouldn't go unnoticed, even if Latin American governments largely have remained silent as the U.S., Europe and Asia increasingly spar over the rules of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  game.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the cause of greater competitiveness in Asia than in Latin America is a little more complicated than just the exchange rate," said Sidney Weintraub, a Latin America economist at the Center for Strategic and International Studies.  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, many Latin American authorities "would welcome some appreciation of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," added Weintraub, who's based in Washington.  \par \par \uc2 -By Mike Esterl, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4026; mike.esterl@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030924dz9o000pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
