\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: HSBC's Gulliver Says Revalued Yuan May Not Rise \b0\par\par\uc2  By James T. Areddy \par \uc2 1,108 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 04:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- The reason China shouldn't revalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par according to the head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading for giant banking\par group HSBC Holdings PLC, is simple: it might not rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "Why would you want to be buying this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? It's not a surefire, slam-dunk," said Stuart Gulliver, chief executive of corporate, investment banking and markets for the London-based giant. \par \par \uc2  He told Dow Jones Newswires late Wednesday that with his 23 years dealing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and 4,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers around the world, HSBC could probably make a quick buck trading a free-floating yuan. Yet, the uncertainty unleashed by such a move would be risky for far more than just China's massive reform agenda. \par \par \uc2  A move would also have ramifications for China's reform process as well as possibly U.S. interest rates, he said. And that could damage the broader prospects of HSBC, a bank which traces its roots to Shanghai and now claims the largest network of branches of a foreign bank in China. \par \par \uc2  "It sounds like motherhood and apple pie...but that's what this is really about," said the 42-year-old banker. \par \par \uc2  Instead, Gulliver said Beijing has wiggle room to make adjustments to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including tying the yuan to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or at least allow it to move in a wider range. Still, he said these moves might not be enough to head off moves by other countries to levy tariffs on Chinese goods. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    China May Have To Make Some Adjustments\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Unlike countries with free-floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , China's capital\par controls limit the influence of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders can\par bring to bear on its exchange rate. Yet, just the same way as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders have agitated for government policy change in other\par countries with big \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bets, Beijing is now feeling the heat in the\par global political arena to adjust its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to respond to calls from\par its biggest trading partners to adjust the yuan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Doing the most strong-arming is the U.S., where weak economic recovery\par coupled with continued job losses are being blamed on a new\par competitiveness from China that critics say is grounded in a weak yuan.\par While many see the yuan as undervalued, HSBC's Gulliver and some others\par say the economics aren't compelling that the yuan would necessarily\par remain strong. And so far, Beijing has put the broader issue of its -\par and what it says is the world's - economic stability ahead of calls to\par revalue.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gulliver, a British national, said his views aren't much different\par than the line from Beijing. "You pick all of this apart and you really\par just want to leave it alone," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But unlike in the late 1990s when it paid global dividends when China\par left the yuan completely unchanged, this time, he says China may have to\par make some adjustments.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among the steps Beijing may take is to set future movements of the\par yuan against a trade-weighted basket, he said. Although perhaps less\par transparent than a basic peg to the dollar, a basket would involve\par adjusting the yuan's exchange rate when the country's balance of trade\par changes with the U.S., Japan and the EU. A basket is considered credible\par by some experts who say it would be a natural exchange rate level,\par instead of the straightforward but somewhat arbitrary dollar peg.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China might also, the HSBC executive said, let the yuan fluctuate in a\par slightly wider range, or band. By buying and selling dollar on\par Shanghai's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market every day, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is\par the most active player and that has ensured since 1994 the yuan's\par exchange rate has had virtually no flexibility around its 8.28 level per\par U.S. dollar. Gulliver said a wider band would be "purely practical,"\par reducing the relatively constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  required by the People's\par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "Disastrous" To Float Yuan\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gulliver cautioned against "simplistic" thinking about the yuan,\par saying it would be "disastrous" for China to float it. Even tinkering\par with the exchange rate level would merely feed speculation of further\par changes, he said. "Once you re-peg, we'd be having this debate every\par week," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On this count, he said the market for the non-deliverable forward\par contracts in the yuan can be "a barometer of sentiment." But there is a\par limited usefulness to such a market, which he said is "not big and\par deep."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Such changes as moving toward a basket or wider band won't reflect the\par political pressure so much as a need to address imbalances in the\par country's financial system where M2 money supply is running around 170%\par of gross domestic product, which he said is among the highest in the\par world.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  inflows - some due to expectations the yuan will rise\par - are threatening some sectors of the Chinese economy with overheating,\par such as the Shanghai property market where prices are rising as banks\par see little reason to turn off the lending taps. The risk that the\par inflows breed inflation and help push Chinese interest rates up are also\par making it difficult for Beijing to sell domestic debt securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gulliver says these factors can be addressed with "prescriptive\par measures" such as taxes on properties or new administrative limits on\par lending to developers that offset the inflationary pressure showing up\par in money supply data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gulliver and others blame signs that China is overheating, where they\par exist, mostly on a poorly developed financial system, which they say\par won't be helped much by changes in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  system. Fixed asset\par investment is over 42% of gross domestic product, and much of the\par investment is misallocated by Chinese banks into nonproductive sectors\par such as property, he said. And even with the liquidity, there is\par outright "stagnation" for hundreds of millions of China's poor, which\par suggests targeted efforts to snuff out overheated sectors, Gulliver\par added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Furthermore, if a rise in the yuan were to halt some foreign\par investment flows that would reduce the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ability - marked\par now by US$360 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves - to buy U.S.\par Treasurys. Heavy buying of Treasurys by foreigners, led by Japan and\par China, helps keep U.S. interest rates low, and any change in this\par dynamic and "the cost of 10-year capital for U.S. firms goes up," he\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By James T. Areddy; Dow Jones Newswires; +86 1370 1700 594;\par james.areddy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Edited by Lim Mui Khi\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030918dz9i0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
