\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-IMF warns on dlr,developing Asia should help bear pain.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Edmonds  \par \uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2003\par 09:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  DUBAI, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar may be in for further weakness and developing Asia is better equipped than Europe or Japan to bear the economic pain of its fall, the International Monetary Fund said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  The Washington-based global lender added its voice - albeit cautiously - to a chorus of European and U.S. officials by saying developing Asian countries should move to free up exchange rates.   \par \par \uc2  In its semi-annual World Economic Outlook, the IMF said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in developing Asia would cut the risk of future crises in the region, make domestic growth less subject to global economic fortunes, lower the carrying cost of official reserves and boost local buying power.   \par \par \uc2  Top European finance chiefs last weekend urged \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  - and Japan, which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep its yen from rising - to stop holding \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates artificially low but there were signs on Wednesday that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted to reduce some of the pressure on China.   \par \par \uc2  Sources said ECB President Wim Duisenberg wanted to turn the spotlight back on to the United States, leader of a high-profile campaign for China to float the yuan, and get America to tackle its gigantic trade and budget deficits.   \par \par \uc2  The IMF said that developing Asia was in a better position economically to buffer the dollar's depreciation because of relatively strong growth and current account surpluses and urged "greater upward exchange rate flexibility" for the region.   \par \par \uc2  YEN RISE COULD HURT JAPAN   \par \par \uc2  However, it had different guidance for Japan, saying an excessive yen rise could hurt Japanese growth by hurting corporate profits, investment, exports and weigh on prices.   \par \par \uc2  The fund also said the easy monetary policy Japan must maintain to tackle deflation suggested the yen may fall further.   \par \par \uc2  Nor was the decline in the U.S. greenback - up to now relatively orderly and, according to the fund, "generally welcome" - necessarily finished.   \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rate adjustments over the past 18 months are in the direction needed to reduce such (current account) imbalances, although the dollar still appears substantially overvalued from a longer-term perspective - implying the likelihood of further downward adjustments," the IMF said.   \par \par \uc2  DLR MAY FALL SHARPLY   \par \par \uc2  A further sharp dollar depreciation, which the IMF said "cannot be ruled out", would have significant implications for the global outlook, particularly if the euro area continues to bear the brunt of the effects.   \par \par \uc2  Later, IMF chief economist Kenneth Rogoff told a news briefing on the WEO that the yawning U.S. current account gap must eventually unwind, and that this would cause a sharp fall in the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  "Some day the U.S. current account deficit ... has to unwind and when it does there will be sharp drop in the dollar," Rogoff said.   \par \par \uc2  More flexible exchange rates in developing Asia would help redress global trade imbalances as well as improve domestic growth in the region.   \par \par \uc2  "With the U.S. dollar's decline necessitating a rebalancing of global demand, the need to boost the domestic component of growth in Asia is even more pressing," the IMF said.   \par \par \uc2  CHINA FACES CALLS TO LOOSEN PEG   \par \par \uc2  China is sure to face calls from Group of Seven finance ministers, meeting on Saturday on the sidelines of the IMF meetings, to loosen its peg of about 8.3 yuan to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  The IMF took a gentler tack.   \par \par \uc2  "The strength of the external position, the desirability of gearing monetary policy toward domestic stabilization objectives and the need to facilitate adjustment to structural changes over the medium-term underscore the importance of moving gradually to greater exchange rate flexibility," it said.   \par \par \uc2  European and U.S. exporters complain bitterly that the low level of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurts their competitiveness and have pressed their political leaders for action.   \par \par \uc2  The IMF meetings officially kick off on September 23, but events surrounding them begin this week.   \par \par \uc2  Europe and the United States have pressed China to relax the dollar peg, allowing the yuan to rise against the U.S. dollar, and while Chinese officials have agreed they will eventually liberalize the system, they have not offered a timetable.   \par \par \uc2  IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler has said the fund should not be lumped into "a coordinated" pressure on China.   \par \par \uc2  The fund's Chief Economist Kenneth Rogoff told Reuters last month the timing of such a shift for China was a delicate matter because of China's fragile banking system. He also warned that it wasn't clear that the big countries' desire for a weaker yuan would be answered over the medium-term.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030918dz9i001ci\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
