\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Watch: Tokyo Has  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Excuses Ready For G7 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2 1,091 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2003\par 12:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese economic officials traveling to Dubai this\par weekend will carry with them a checklist of reasons why it's fine for\par Tokyo to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market but not for\par Beijing to do the same.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  They will have those arguments ready at the Dubai meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the Group of Seven in case their G7 counterparts criticize Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy views. \par \par \uc2  It is an awkward set of beliefs: on the one hand, Tokyo thinks it is necessary to spend record amounts in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen from rising and snuffing out an economic recovery by weighing on exports. But on the other hand, those officials believe the government of China, a rapidly growing economy but one that is still much poorer than Japan, ought to relax its firm grip on the yuan and let the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen. \par \par \uc2  Some G7 officials, most notably U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, argue that it is best for governments and central banks to stay out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and they might therefore not agree fully with Tokyo's contention that Japan needs to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should ease off. \par \par \uc2  So, Tokyo policy-makers are going to Dubai prepared for debate. And once the G7 show is over, they are likely to step back into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as needed to stem a yen rise. In fact, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are on alert for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the meeting because the yen has strengthened to around Y116.00 to the dollar - a level at which Japan has entered the market in the past. \par \par \uc2  Japan won't have its finance minister present at the Dubai meeting, because the 81-year old Masajuro Shiokawa, still recovering from an illness, has decided to skip it. Instead, representing Japan will be Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui and Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi, and their script for justifying a weak yen and a stronger yuan appears to be in place. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Action To Curb Volatility\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Tokyo policy-makers could first argue that yen-selling\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is aimed at curbing excessive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate volatility,\par rather than guiding the yen to a specific level which would be\par advantageous to Japan's exporters.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Fluctuations that are too big are undesirable," Prime Minister\par Junichiro Koizumi said Tuesday. "It's important to make (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) stable."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of course, Tokyo isn't blind to exchange rate levels. And Y115.00 to\par the dollar is considered a particularly sensitive technical and\par psychological level that Japan's Finance Ministry and the BOJ are\par dead-set on defending, because a dollar break below it could lead to a\par free-fall.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The way dollar/yen options are set up now, "any move, particularly to\par the downside, will be accelerated by options hedging activity,"\par according to Marshall Gittler, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in\par Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There's also the question of economic fundamentals, the policy-makers\par may say at Dubai.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par True, Japan's real gross domestic product grew at a blazing annualized\par rate of 3.9% in the April-June quarter, outpacing growth in the U.S. and\par Europe.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the negative factors that had been dragging down Japan's economy\par for years - falling prices, sluggish private consumption, mounds of bad\par debt in the banking sector - haven't disappeared.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the finance ministry's international bureau,\par said he doesn't see much change in the underlying fundamentals of Japan\par and the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "For the moment, Japan's (second-quarter) 3.9% figure is highest (when\par compared to the U.S. or Europe). But that doesn't mean Japan's economy\par is the strongest," he said. "Fundamentals are something that refers to\par something a little more stable."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             China Economy Outshines Japan's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And China's economy continues to outshine the much-bigger Japanese\par economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While Japan's consumer price index has fallen for almost four years\par straight, China's CPI has risen eight months in a row, and deflationary\par worries have been replaced by inflation concerns in the world's most\par populous country. China's GDP also zoomed 6.7% higher on year in second\par quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In case anyone forgot, Japan is still suffering from deflation,"\par while China is not, said a Japanese government official.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo officials could argue that a weaker yen is one policy option to\par overcome falling prices at a time when Japan can't go much further with\par fiscal stimulus due to the huge government debt, and has little room to\par take more monetary steps with interest rates near zero.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, China's yuan is expected to come up as an important topic\par at the Dubai meeting of the G7 countries, of which China isn't a member.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's been a lot of debate on the Chinese yuan, starting with the\par Paris G7 meeting in February, leading up to the APEC meeting this\par month," said a senior Japanese finance ministry official. "G7\par discussions will follow that flow of events."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Such talks are likely to prompt Washington to call on China to allow\par the yuan to move more freely against the dollar, an opinion with which\par Japan agrees and European G7 members might also sympathize with as\par Chinese exports to the region climb.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In fact, Takatoshi Kato, a former vice finance minister for\par international affairs in Japan and now an adviser to the Bank of\par Tokyo-Mitsubishi, said the G7's post-meeting statement on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might reflect such discussions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, those talks about letting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates be determined by the\par market could circle back to Japan, leading to Japan's G7 counterparts to\par question whether Tokyo needs to exert so much of an effort to keeping\par the yen weak against the dollar and the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Finance Minister Shiokawa, for one, said Tuesday that Japan's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy isn't expected to be a major issue at the G7\par because, "we've explained our position thoroughly in the past."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And the Japanese officials have many excuses to fall back on regarding\par why they need to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , though it is up the other G7 countries to\par buy or brush off those arguments, Japanese government officials say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Miyako Takebe, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and government policy for\par Dow Jones Newswires in Tokyo, has been a financial journalist in Japan\par for seven years.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1187 4256 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030917dz9h000od\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
