\par\par \b \uc2 Daily Chinese yuan update - Sept 17.  \b0\par\par\uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2003\par 12:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Sept 17 (Reuters) - There follows a summary of market activity and news articles relating to the Chinese yuan on Wednesday:  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS  \par \par \uc2 *** China's yuan ended unchanged versus the U.S. dollar at 8.2775 on Wednesday, at the stronger end of its managed trading range.  \par \par \uc2 *** One-year non-deliverable forwards were traded around 1,970 points at 0850 GMT on Wednesday after Tuesday's close at a record 2,130 points, implying a price of 8.065 yuan per dollar in a year's time.  \par \par \uc2 China is likely to face calls at Saturday's Group of Seven meeting in Dubai to let its yuan appreciate, with the United States in particular worried that its peg at about 8.3 to the dollar means Chinese exports are artificially cheap.  \par \par \uc2 NDFs are a transaction where a forward price is agreed between a customer and a bank, but settlement on the value date is undertaken entirely in U.S. dollars.  \par \par \uc2 *** One-year implied yuan volatility hit a two-year high of 5.50 percent on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Implied volatility is a measure of how much the options market expects the price of the underlying asset to move during the life of the option. Volatilities are often traded actively and independently.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan moves in a band of 8.2760 to 8.2800 enforced by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Turnover rose but was still thin at $450 million from $400 million on Tuesday. The yuan softened to 7.1188 against 100 Japanese yen from 7.0970 but firmed versus the euro to 9.2427 from 9.3406.  \par \par \uc2 OPEN MARKET ACTIVITY  \par \par \uc2 The central People's Bank of China conducted weekly open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday. It issued 40 billion yuan in short-term bills to mop up money supply.  \par \par \uc2 NEWS  \par \par \uc2 ** Canada says G7 to discuss China, Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 OTTAWA - Finance ministers from the Group of Seven rich industrialized nations are expected to discuss the Chinese yuan and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tied to it when they meet in Dubai, United Arab Emirates, on Sept. 20-22, Canadian Finance Minister John Manley said on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Full story:  \par \par \uc2 ** China cancels T-bond issue in unusual move  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI - In a rare move, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it had cancelled a treasury bond issue due to irregular interest rate movements on the country's money market.  \par \par \uc2 Full story:  \par \par \uc2 ** China curbs QFII dollar inflows to fight hot money  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI - China has begun curbing dollar investment inflows under a scheme to let foreign players into its domestic markets, which analysts said on Wednesday was the country's latest attempt to relieve upward pressure on the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Full story:  \par \par \uc2 ** Asian options volatility rises ahead of G7  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE - Implied volatilities on most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options shot up ahead of a Group of Seven meeting at the weekend, where the region's policy of keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in check is expected to be debated. The one-year implied volatility on the Chinese yuan options rose to 5.50 percent, a two-year high.  \par \par \uc2 Full story:  \par \par \uc2 ** Growth, not criticism, key to Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE - Financial markets might be positioning for it, but Asia's central banks are unlikely to bow to U.S. and European demands to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until they are convinced of the global upturn. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will eventually allow a more flexible yuan, but its priority is domestic stability, not appeasing foreign complaints.  \par \par \uc2 Full story:.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030917dz9h001ta\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
