\par\par \b \uc2  IMF: Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserves Buildup In Many Cases Too Much \b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2003\par 20:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- Asia's massive buildup of foreign reserves needs to slow if the region is to take on a fair share of the burden of adjusting to a narrower U.S. trade deficit, the International Monetary Fund said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Although a high level of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves can help protect against financial crisis, many Asian governments have accumulated reserves far beyond what is needed to cover short-term debt. The cost of holding excessive reserves has several aspects, running from the relatively low interest rates earned on the reserves to the risk of provoking protectionist pressures in trade deficit countries. \par \par \uc2  "The rapid buildup of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in some emerging economies is understandable from a number of perspectives, but may now be approaching the point where some slowdown in the rate of accumulation is desirable," the IMF said in an essay included in its latest World Economic Outlook released Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Central banks in China, Japan, South Korea, and Taiwan expanded their official dollar holdings by $28 billion in July. Together these four countries now hold $1.21 trillion of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - half of which has been bought since 1999. China holds over $350 billion worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Taiwan's reserves are roughly $180 billion, or equal to 10 times its short-term debt. In 2003 so far, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought $86 billion U.S. dollars, boosting reserves to over $500 billion. \par \par \uc2  Governments raise foreign reserves either by purchasing hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on international markets, or by running a current account surplus. The current account is a broad measure of trade flows, taking into account both goods and services trade and capital flows. \par \par \uc2  "While exchange rate depreciation can help boost external demand in the aftermath of a crisis, it eventually becomes important to shift to domestic demand sources of growth," the IMF said. An undervalued exchange rate can hurt growth by reducing consumption and lowering domestic investment, the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, a properly valued exchange rate allows economic players to respond to productivity growth and facilitates trade. \par \par \uc2  As a region with highly open economies, Asia has a great interest in a smooth rebalancing of the U.S. current account deficit. For several years, U.S. demand has outpaced that of the rest of the world, fueling imports as export growth lagged. Most economists say the U.S. probably can't continue to attract enough foreign capital to continue financing such large deficits. If foreign demand for U.S. goods doesn't pick up and reduce the deficit, and foreigners lend less capital to the U.S., higher interest rates could be the result. \par \par \uc2  "An eventual narrowing of the U.S. current account deficit from its present unsustainable level will likely require emerging economies in Asia to share in the adjustment, to prevent an undue burden of adjustment on other countries," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  A weaker U.S. dollar could promote more balanced trade for the U.S., the IMF said, but so far the price adjustment has fallen exclusively on the euro area. \par \par \uc2  "Emerging economies in Asia have a strong interest in the smooth rotation of demand from the U.S. to other parts of the world, to ensure that global growth does not slow unnecessarily and to maintain the orderly adjustment of exchange rates, not least to keep protectionist pressures at bay," the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF urged Asian countries which manage floating exchange rates through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less. Countries which peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notably \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , should gradually move to greater flexibility, the IMF said. \par \par \uc2  -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030911dz9b008ya\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
