\par\par \b \uc2  Most Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Dn Late; Dollar Steady Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2003\par 12:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened Friday as the dollar's steady performance against the yen set a firm tone for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region. \par \par \uc2  Concerns that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for a second day in a row to cap the yen's strength kept the dollar bears in line, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The MOF bought an estimated $5 billion Thursday to prop up the dollar after it fell to a 15-week low against its Japanese counterpart. \par \par \uc2  Market players believe the MOF will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a strong yen from stifling signs of improvement in the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  "The move ... signaled to markets that Japan maintains an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance despite ongoing discussions with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pursue more flexible exchange rates" at a meeting of Asia-Pacific finance ministers in Phuket, Thailand, United Overseas Bank said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded in a narrow range of Y116.75 to Y116.90 Friday. It settled around Y116.80 near the end of Asian trading, up from Y116.70 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, Thai baht, Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso all slipped against the dollar, while the South Korean won and New Taiwan dollar strengthened. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar fell to a one-month low in early trading before trimming its losses near the end of the session. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian trading the dollar was quoted at S$1.7580, up from S$1.7558 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar spiked to its session high of S$1.7615 in the first hour of trading as it gathered strength from its gains against the yen. It was the U.S. dollar's best performance in more than a month. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, some market players were wary of holding long dollar positions ahead of the weekend, selling the U.S. unit as the day progressed. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, market participants expect limited upside for the Singapore dollar, given the possibility of further monetary easing when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes its policy statement next month. \par \par \uc2  Corporate dollar demand, combined with persistent political and security concerns in the Philippines, weighed on the peso. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 55.02 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP54.95 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the peso was unable to sustain gains made early this week as companies bought dollars to pay for imports and cover external debt obligations. \par \par \uc2  South Korean corporations stocked up on the won ahead of next week's Chuseok holiday, which will keep financial markets closed for three straight days. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at KRW1,170.6, down from KRW1,173.1 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  Continued foreign equity inflows also underpinned the won. Foreign investors were net buyers of local shares for the 14th straight session. \par \par \uc2  Chuseok, the South Korean Thanksgiving, will be celebrated Sept. 10-12. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar strengthened modestly on exporter selling of U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$34.117, compared with NT$34.120 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped mute the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Trading was relatively calm, in part due to the narrow trading range of the U.S. dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah lost ground after holding steady against the dollar for three straight sessions. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday the dollar was quoted at IDR8,495, up from IDR8,480 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign players covered some of their dollar-short positions that they had built earlier this week as the dollar staged rallies against most other regional rivals. \par \par \uc2  The short-covering pushed the dollar to an intraday high of IDR8,520 before fresh selling emerged. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht, the dollar was quoted at THB40.775 in late Asian trading, compared with THB40.770 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar climbed to THB40.890 in morning trading, tracking its gains against the yen. \par \par \uc2  However, exporters became dollar sellers as the U.S. unit approached the key THB40.90 level, causing it to reverse course. \par \par \uc2  The dollar continued to languish in the afternoon, pressured by equity-linked inflows. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 9862-0905; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Kevin Lim and Bernice Tang \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030905dz95000p3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
