\par\par \b \uc2  China Denies Manipulating Yuan, Denounces Intl Pressure \b0\par\par\uc2 745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2003\par 17:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING (AP)--China isn't manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and won't not give in to "international browbeating" to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, the government's main English newspaper said Tuesday as the U.S. Treasury Secretary arrived for a visit. \par \par \uc2  The China Daily, in its top opinion piece, took issue with critics who say the country is pulling strings with the yuan in a protectionist attempt to help Chinese-based exporters sell inexpensive products. \par \par \uc2  "The critics that believe China manipulates the yuan's value assume that every \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world should be floated in the market," China Daily said. "This assumption in itself is porous." \par \par \uc2  It added: "Should China now give in to pressure only to face dire consequences later? No way." \par \par \uc2  The commentary, in a forum considered China's primary English-language conduit to world opinion, came as U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow began a two-day visit to Beijing. The yuan issue was expected to come up during a meeting with Zhou Xiaochuan, China's central banker. \par \par \uc2  Snow made no comment Tuesday as he arrived at his Beijing hotel other than to say he expects talks with Chinese officials to go well. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, White House spokesman Scott McClellan said Snow would raise the issue of freeing the yuan, saying such discussions represented "progress in and of itself." \par \par \uc2  "China has indicated in the past that they intend to move toward greater flexibility and this is something we support and encourage. It is our view that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values are best set in open and competitive markets with \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept to a minimum," McClellan said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept the yuan - known also as the renminbi, or "people's money" - fixed at about 8.28 to the U.S. dollar since 1994. It is allowed to fluctuate, but only in a tight band - a fraction of 1% - and only in closely regulated trading by those who have cleared official hurdles. \par \par \uc2  In Japan on Monday, Snow and Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa agreed to encourage - but not pressure - China to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  "China seems to be keeping the yuan fixed and this is not competitive from an international financial viewpoint," Shiokawa said Tuesday in Tokyo. "I agreed with Secretary Snow that China should let the yuan float." \par \par \uc2  In recent weeks, China has taken some steps toward a slightly more flexible exchange rate, including loosening certain capital account restrictions. \par \par \uc2  "It is important we retain flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets so U.S. manufacturers are not disadvantaged," Snow said in Japan. "My long-held view is that a well-functioning international financial system is one based on flexible exchange rates determined in competitive markets." \par \par \uc2  The yuan doubtless will be an important issue later this week at a meeting of the 21-member Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation forum, which will be attended by Snow, Shiokawa and Chinese Finance Minister Jin Renqing. \par \par \uc2  Ahead of that, other state-controlled Chinese-language newspapers also ran pieces Tuesday, apparently part of an attempt to take issue with what China perceives as interference in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. \par \par \uc2  For nearly a decade, Beijing has kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from trading in global markets - a strategy that brought stability and helped shield China from the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis. But as exports surge, China is facing growing demands to raise the value of the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Washington and other governments complain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is too cheap and gives Chinese exports an unfair advantage, hurting foreign companies and wiping out jobs in countries trying to compete with China. \par \par \uc2  Officials in China say they have no immediate plans to alter the exchange rate or let the yuan trade freely. But they are looking at ways to overhaul \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls to help the country's businesses and to lessen foreign pressure for a stronger yuan. \par \par \uc2  China Daily said a country retains the right to choose how it handles its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that "international browbeating" cannot effectively mandate policy. What's more, it said, China's financial policies remain wise in a globalized and uncertain financial landscape. \par \par \uc2  "What will happen if China allows the renminbi to appreciate now? Will American manufacturers, who cry hardest on this issue, benefit from a revalued yuan? Not likely," the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  But, it said, "a more expensive renminbi will artificially hurt the competitiveness of China's exports and smash the rice bowls of the workers whose livelihoods rely on trade." \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030902dz920010g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
