\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Snow,Shiokawa say not forcing  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  policy on China.   \b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2003\par 16:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 1 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow agreed with Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa China should not be forced to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said late on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Snow was in Tokyo on Monday to meet Japanese officials, then goes to Beijing from Tuesday where he meets the head of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The United States and Japan have urged China to revalue the yuan, which is pegged in a narrow range around 8.28 to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "We agreed that China should think what is best for themselves. We will tell the Chinese what we think but we will not force it upon China," said the official who was briefing reporters on the talks between Snow and Shiokawa.   \par \par \uc2 Snow has held talks with a number of top Japanese economic and political officials during a visit here. He earlier repeated his belief flexible exchange rates were best for the global financial system.   \par \par \uc2 The official said Snow and Shiokawa did not discuss specific \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves that have occurred in the market recently. Shiokawa told Snow that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect fundamentals and that excessive movements were undesirable.   \par \par \uc2 "They agreed that their views are similar," the MOF official said.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. companies complain that Japan keeps its yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially low by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent its appreciation, while \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg has the same effect of giving its products an unfair trade advantage.   \par \par \uc2 BUT PRESSURE MOUNTS   \par \par \uc2 Pressure is mounting nevertheless on China and other Asian countries to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise to help adjust the U.S. current account gap, which Snow said could weigh on the dollar if it rises further.   \par \par \uc2 Horst Koehler, managing director of the International Monetary Fund called on Monday for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate flexibility in Asia, echoing views of Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg.   \par \par \uc2 Snow earlier met members of Japan's biggest business lobby and was quoted as saying he wanted China to adopt flexible trade and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies.   \par \par \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda, formerly Japan's top financial diplomat and now a special adviser to the cabinet, said earlier on Monday Beijing should phase in a revaluation of the yuan to ensure a smooth transition.   \par \par \uc2 But China stood firm behind its pegged yuan rate, with the state-run Xinhua news agency saying some countries have attributed their economic problems to China's yuan policy and the criticism from overseas was unfair.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030901dz91003j6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
