\par\par \b \uc2  MONUMENT SECURITIES: Mr Shiokawa Fails To Move Beijing \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stephen Lewis \par \uc2 1,063 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2003\par 16:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of Monument Securities \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- Mr Shiokawa pressed at last week's ASEAN+3 meeting for the Chinese authorities to revalue the yuan renminbi.  His entreaties were rebuffed.  It was unlikely that Chinese leaders would yield to foreign pressure, especially from within an organisation that is seen by many regional leaders as a forerunner of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc to rival the dollar bloc and the euro zone.  Prime Minister Mahathir of Malaysia, for example, last week called for ASEAN+3 to reconstitute itself formally as an Asian Economic Union.  The problem is there is no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such a bloc around which the others would naturally gravitate.  The US dollar has dominated its bloc.  In the euro zone, there was a focus, prior to the establishment of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the Deutschemark; other central banks tended to take their lead from the Bundesbank.  In East Asia, though, the question would be whether the Japanese yen or the Chinese yuan was to be the rallying-point for the formation of the bloc.  There is potentially a great deal at stake, economically and politically, in how this question is answered.  The difficulty of finding a way forward may, indeed, defeat attempts to form an Asian economic area.  In these circumstances, China's leadership is unlikely to take instruction from Japanese ministers on how it should manage the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  Mr Shiokawa's initiative has probably been counter-productive. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, there is a significant body of international opinion that regards China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy as constituting an unfair trade practice. In one industry after another, China's competitors are finding their own output displaced by low-priced Chinese products.  All the while, in pegging their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the US dollar, the Chinese authorities are buying dollars for yuan.  As a measure of the scale of these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by $102bn in the year to end-May (the latest period for which IMF data is available). They have been massive.  If \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to desist from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float higher, or if it revalued its exchange rate to a level where there was no longer an imbalance between supply and demand for the yuan, its exports would lose some of their competitive edge.  China's policies would then be less disruptive of trade and production patterns elsewhere in the world. That is the burden of the argument against Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  China's leaders could mount a defence, however.  IMF figures show the yuan's real effective exchange rate in the early months of this year was still not down to its 1995 average.  Though China is in the black on trade account, the surplus appears not to be growing.  Chinese exports are expanding rapidly but so are imports. The problem is that Chinese exports are skewed towards consumer and intermediate goods, whereas imports are concentrated in capital goods.  Consequently, foreign industries that are facing fierce Chinese competition are not the ones that are seeing an offset from increased sales into China.  In raising the external value of the yuan, the Chinese authorities would reduce the cost of the goods they need to import to sustain capital investment but would penalise the output from Chinese companies on world markets.  This would heighten the risk of a glut of goods developing in the internal market.  Beijing's policymakers can also argue that it ill becomes the Japanese finance minister to lecture them on the shortcomings of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy when Japan is pursuing a similar strategy, only on an even grander scale.  Japan is likewise holding down the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through heavy market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  Indeed, in the twelve months to May, the Bank of Japan amassed an additional $120bn of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  The Japanese trade surplus actually increased last year, from $70bn to $94bn, reflecting a rise in exports and a fall in imports. Yet, there is no strong international pressure on Japan to allow the yen to float.  On the contrary, the US Treasury appears well content that Japan has more or less fixed the yen-dollar exchange rate, if only because this will help to stabilise portfolio flows between Japan and the USA.  In the case of Japan, both the official and the private sectors are significant holders of US securities whereas, in China, US financial assets are largely held by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  The Bank of China could not switch out of these assets without weakening the US dollar and thereby putting strain on its own exchange rate policy.  In practical terms, therefore, the US authorities have nothing to gain from yuan/dollar stability to parallel the clear advantages they derive from a steady yen/dollar rate. \par \par \uc2  The Chinese authorities may well believe they have been singled out unfairly for criticism.  They seem to recognise there may be an internal case for revaluing the yuan.  The reason why their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is strong is not primarily the impact of the trade surplus.  It reflects continuous inflows of capital that also boost liquidity in domestic money markets. Some Beijing officials have spoken tentatively of examining the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the dollar peg.  However, international pressure may be hardening Chinese policymakers against exchange rate action.  Today, they have announced that, instead, they will deal with the liquidity problem by making a record bill issue of Yuan 50bn tomorrow to soak up surplus funds. \par \par \uc2  -By Stephen Lewis: 44 20 7338 0179: analysismonumentsecurities.com \par \par \uc2  (Stephen Lewis is chief economist at Monument Securities Ltd., London, independent brokers specializing in institutional business.) \par \par \uc2  Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  This column is published for information only, and it neither constitutes, nor is to be construed as, an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources the author believes to be reliable, but he cannot represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd., employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this article. Monument Securities Ltd. is regulated by the SFA and is a member of the London Stock Exchange, LIFFE and ISMA. \par \par \uc2  1089 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030811dz8b003e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
