\par\par \b \uc2  China Seeks To Ease Pressure For Revaluation Of Yuan - FT \b0\par\par\uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2003\par 02:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- China is looking at ways to relieve pressure for the revaluation of the yuan, as a prelude to adjusting the 10-year-old system that effectively pegs the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar, the Financial Times reported on its Web site Wednesday, citing officials and academics. \par \par \uc2  Such considerations have become more urgent recently as a rapid inflow of "hot money" has strained the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to dictate monetary policy. Officials said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had recently been forced to buy an average of $600 million dollars a day to steady the exchange rate, the FT reported. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to drive \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves above $340 billion by the end of June, up from $316 billion at the end of March. This figure - which has not been officially announced - may reinforce the view that the yuan is undervalued. \par \par \uc2  The yuan's future is important in Asian countries, partly because any revaluation would be seen as a possible trigger for a round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciations throughout the region. \par \par \uc2  The increase in reserves is due less to export competitiveness than to expectations by Chinese companies that the yuan will appreciate. "They are bringing their funds back to China and changing them into renminbi in expectation of a revaluation," one official said, according to the FT. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, is also known as the renminbi. \par \par \uc2  Given such strains, the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is considering ways of limiting the growth in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - a policy departure for a government that has seen its large reserves as a symbol of national pride. One option under consideration would be to allow companies to keep a larger portion of their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings, either within China or offshore. \par \par \uc2  All but a fraction of such earnings must now be sold to the PBOC, adding to the reserves and boosting domestic money supply. \par \par \uc2  Another option would be gradually to relax restrictions on Chinese people and companies wishing to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  There are strict limits on how many - and for what purpose - U.S. dollars can be bought by Chinese in exchange for yuan. In addition, Chinese companies are to be encouraged to invest abroad, both directly and in financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Domestic companies will be allowed to buy bonds in overseas markets and the authorities are considering allowing selected institutional investors to trade stocks in Hong Kong, the FT reported. \par \par \uc2  There is no timetable for the adoption of any of the options under consideration, officials said, and timing may depend on whether revaluation pressure abates or intensifies. \par \par \uc2  Only when the PBOC is comfortable that demand for yuan and dollars is more closely aligned will it allow market forces greater play in determining the yuan's value against the U.S. dollar, the officials added. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030702dz7200009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
