\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Resist gains, watching China, Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2003\par 11:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, June 16 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stalled\par after early gains on Monday as traders fretted about possible\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the erosion in export\par competitiveness against the Chinese yuan and the yen's\par reluctance to rally.\par     The opening gains came after the U.S. dollar was weakened\par by soft U.S. data that heightened expectations of a U.S.\par interest rate cut later this month.\par     The Singapore dollar        rose over a cent from Friday's\par lows, the Indonesian rupiah  was back on an uptrend that\par stalled in late May, the Korean won             tested\par three-month onshore highs and the Thai baht  rose to a\par 10-day peak.\par     But as markets speculated over Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cap\par the yen and placed bets on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yielding to pressure to widen\par the yuan's tight trading band, regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dithered.\par     "Asia wants to join the trend of a weak U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar and, at\par the same time, Asian central banks want to make their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par competitive mainly due to the fact that despite the weaker\par dollar, China still keeps its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pegged," said Frank\par Gong, head of Asian markets strategy at Bank of America.\par  \par     China has become an increasingly important trading partner\par for many Asian economies, Gong said.\par     "And, of course, the Bank of Japan has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par hold the dollar-yen up. Both of those factors are limiting how\par much Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can strengthen," Gong said.\par     Yuan revaluation trades, which bet on the Chinese yuan\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par being allowed to appreciate from the 8.276-8.28 range it now\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par keeps to against the dollar, have picked up in the past week,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with one-year forward yuan premiums in the non-deliverable\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par market rising to a historic high of 1,500 points .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar was restricted after its early gains\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by expectations of investment outflows and the risk of\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore ahead of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par next month's semi-annual policy review.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Philippine peso  opened firmer after a holiday\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par weekend, boosted by bunched dollar inflows and the regional\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par trend, but then stalled.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said authorities were suspected to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in Taiwan, where the Taiwan dollar's  gains halted\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par around 34.60 - still its strongest local close in three\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The rupiah could not progress much beyond 8,185 and the\par Thai baht met resistance even before touching 41.50.\par     Still, Gong said, South East Asia was less susceptible to\par the pressures of keeping pace with the yen.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the baht were backed by strong\par fundamentals, while the rupiah had the appeal of its high\par yield.\par     "South East Asia, in general, would have a better chance to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par strengthen against the U.S. dollar because they are influenced\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par less compared to North East Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by dollar-yen and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the Chinese renminbi," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen-won cross rate was steady at 10.12 ,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par showing the won's gains were limited while the yen was capped.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0835 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     117.51       117.60         +0.08\par  Sing dlr      1.7287       1.7325         +0.22\par  Taiwan dlr    34.599       34.645         +0.13\par  Korean won   1190.10      1191.90         +0.15\par  Baht           41.54        41.59         +0.12\par  Peso           53.40        53.61         +0.39\par  Rupiah       8195.00      8215.00         +0.24\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     117.51       118.74         +1.05\par  Sing dlr      1.7287       1.7341         +0.31\par  Taiwan dlr    34.599       34.753         +0.45\par  Korean won   1190.10      1185.80         -0.36\par  Baht           41.54        43.08         +3.71\par  Peso           53.40        53.38         -0.04\par  Rupiah       8195.00      8940.00         +9.0