\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA's Yam Says China PBOC Bills Significant For Rates \b0\par\par\uc2 513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2003\par 05:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- The head of Hong Kong's quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the introduction of a bills program by the People's Bank of China is a significant step toward relaxing controls over yuan interest rates, which is considered a precursor to full convertibility of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While applauding the system as "a significant move toward sophistication in monetary management," Joseph Yam, chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said the PBOC system also has room for improvement. \par \par \uc2  "This technical story is very encouraging, not just in terms of the increasing sophistication in monetary management on the mainland, which augers well for the future development and possible liberalization of the (yuan) money market, but also in the very high degree of transparency with which all this is being organized," Yam said in his weekly column published Thursday on the HKMA Web site. \par \par \uc2  Liberalizing yuan money market interest rates, as Yam said the bill offering system could entail, is considered a key step toward full convertibility of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month adjusted the way it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local money markets by introducing a program that involves selling short-term debt instruments bearing its own name to commercial banks. This week, it sold 20 billion yuan ($1=CNY8.28) worth of three- and six-month bills, which mopped up funds in the country's interbank money market. \par \par \uc2  Yam said the PBOC's bill issuance will allow the authority to keep the country's massive foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows from upsetting domestic monetary conditions. \par \par \uc2  If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't buy back some of the yuan it issues to banks that have exchanged U.S. dollars, inflation would build up or official yuan interest rates would have to rise. Yam said this so-called "sterilization" of the inflows is the PBOC's key desire now, but that the new system will also allow the development of a sophisticated money market. \par \par \uc2  How the bill offerings by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help the authority offset the monetary impact of the massive inflow of foreign direct investment and trade receipts was outlined earlier this week in a commentary by Dai Genyou, director of monetary policy at China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dai cited the "big scale change" in the country's balance of payments recently for bringing forward the PBOC's decision to adopt a U.S.-style bill program. \par \par \uc2  Yam said that the PBOC may want to eventually move the bill issue program, which now occurs Tuesdays, to a more frequent timetable "of the type carried out daily by other central banks engaging in discretionary monetary management." \par \par \uc2  Later, the PBOC may find that the "grounds of competitive fairness" may prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop announcing details of how banks bid and prevailed at its bill auctions. "It may be that the transparency in this market is a little overdone," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  -By James T. Areddy, Dow Jones Newswires; (86) 1370 1700 594; james.areddy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Debra Boyce \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030516dz5g0006q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\