\par\par \b \uc2  China in no hurry to drop stable  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  mantra. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Wheatley, Asian Economics Correspondent \par \uc2 1,027 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2002\par 08:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Aug 20 (Reuters) - Few debates in Asian economics generate as much heat as when and how China should introduce greater flexibility to its all-but-fixed exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  But not in Beijing. \par \par \uc2  Japanese businessmen and lawmakers have called for a revaluation of the yuan as have U.S. politicians and union leaders. They are afraid that what they see as a cheap exchange rate and low labour costs make China's export formula unbeatable. \par \par \uc2  And U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill has publicly chided \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  for "defeating the market" by its active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged in a tight range of 8.2760 to 8.2800 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Yet the complaints are like water off a duck's back in Beijing, whose priority is to keep the economy on an even keel while it absorbs the shocks induced by China's accession last December to the World Trade Organisation. A pending reshuffle of China's top leadership also puts a premium on stability. \par \par \uc2  "This year is a very special year for the Chinese government," said Long Guoqiang of the Development Research Centre of the State Council, or cabinet. \par \par \uc2  Reform of the exchange rate system should come later, "perhaps in two to three years", Long, deputy director-general of the Centre's Foreign Economics Relations Department, added. \par \par \uc2  "This year the government's priority is to implement all the WTO commitments. They don't want to have to face more uncertainties," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some sectors, notably cars, are facing a new blast of competition as WTO tariff cuts bring down prices. But officials are relieved that the overall transition has begun smoothly. \par \par \uc2  Official figures last week showed China's imports grew 13.2 percent between January and July to $155.6 billion, lagging 16.2 percent growth in exports to $171.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  The trade surplus over the period jumped 55 percent to $15.6 billion. With foreign direct investment booming, China also enjoys a plump capital account surplus. \par \par \uc2  The result is upward pressure on the exchange rate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offsets by buying dollars. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit a record $242.8 billion at the end of June, an increase of $30.6 billion in the first six months. \par \par \uc2  RIPENESS \par \par \uc2  The stockpile of reserves is dwarfed only by Japan's. But it represents what economists call an opportunity cost because the money, which is typically parked in rich-country bonds, would probably earn more if it were invested in the economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can also make it hard to control the money supply. \par \par \uc2  No wonder then that many investment bankers, sniffing new trading opportunities, argue the time is ripe for a progressive floating of the yuan to better adjust to WTO-related shocks and the eventual liberalisation of capital account transactions. \par \par \uc2  Goldman Sachs calculates that, on a trade-weighted basis, the yuan is about 10 percent undervalued - not enough, the bank says, to create a big drag on growth elsewhere in the region. \par \par \uc2  But Fan Gang, director of the National Economic Research Institute, said the level of China's reserves did not yet pose a problem. He thought the issue of liberalising the exchange rate would not be on the political agenda for one or two years. \par \par \uc2  Fan said the yuan would probably rise if it were floated. But, with China suffering deflation, he added: "As a policy, I don't think China should or will let it appreciate." \par \par \uc2  Long at the Development Research Centre agreed the yuan would rise, not fall, if its trading band were loosened. \par \par \uc2  But for a government whose priority is to find work for 12-15 million people who come on the job market each year, the benefits that exchange rate stability brings China's fast-growing export industries is not an asset to be discarded lightly. \par \par \uc2  "Pro-export policies will be a long-term policy choice by the Chinese government because the majority in the government think our international competitiveness is not strong enough," he said. \par \par \uc2  Li Yushi, vice president of the foreign trade ministry's think tank, echoed that view. He also said China's big pile of reserves had to be measured against its growing import needs, underlined by a $11-14 billion liquefied natural gas supply contract awarded on August 8 to an Australian consortium. \par \par \uc2  "We will need to import a huge amount of advanced technology and natural resources in the next five-10 years," said Li, who expects China's trade surplus to shrink over the next two to three years and then turn, in some years, into a deficit. \par \par \uc2  Li said he shared the consensus among Chinese scholars that, as well as eventually letting the yuan float more freely, China should replace its dollar peg with a more stable trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the euro and the yen. \par \par \uc2  NOT SO QUICK \par \par \uc2  But the consensus, based in part on the experience of the 1997 Asian financial crisis, also holds that China would be courting disaster if it liberalised its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate regime and capital account before it had strengthened its ailing banks and improved its capacity to supervise the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Sure enough, Beijing has said its priority is to beef up the banks, saddled with massive non-performing loans, in preparation for the WTO-mandated entry of foreign banks in five years' time. \par \par \uc2  "Against this political and economic background, a change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime is no longer on the 'must do' list for this year..." said Frank Gong with Bank of America in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  As Long at the Development Research Centre said, there is only so much that any government can accomplish at one time. \par \par \uc2  And this year is special because China is feverishly preparing a five-yearly Communist Party Congress at which party chief Jiang Zemin and other leaders over 70 are due to step down. \par \par \uc2  "Rarely does an administration in any country push through big changes in its final year. So I think it's understandable this year that the government doesn't want to do more," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020820dy8k00cpb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
