\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Marginally Stronger As Dollar Demand Eases \b0\par\par\uc2 248 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2002\par 05:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- China's yuan strengthened marginally against the U.S. dollar Wednesday as dollar buying from importers eased after two days of strong demand. \par \par \uc2  At 0300 GMT (11:00 p.m. EDT Tuesday), the dollar was at 8.2774 yuan, down from Tuesday's CNY8.2775, after trading in a tight range of CNY8.2773 and CNY8.2775 during the session. \par \par \uc2  "(Dollar) demand from importers isn't as strong as it was in the past two days," a trader at a foreign bank in Shanghai said, adding that most transactions were done at CNY8.2774. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit an intraday high of CNY8.2870 Monday, the highest level since March 7 when the intraday high was CNY8.2874. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the dollar to slide to CNY8.2770 next week, as demand eases further. \par \par \uc2  China's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only be traded to cover current account transactions, and most exporters are required to sell 85% of their export receipts to state commercial banks. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monitors the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary, to ensure that the pair trade in a narrow band. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to CNY7.8882 from CNY7.8294. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar edged lower to CNY1.0607 from CNY1.0608, while the Japanese yen was stronger at CNY6.6600 per 100 yen from CNY6.6590 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218-3268; djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji000002