\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-SE Asia beware, China export juggernaut rolls on. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Erin Prelypchan \par \uc2 848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2002\par 10:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, May 24 (Reuters) - China's economy is set to jog comfortably ahead of its Asian neighbours in 2002 as exports are boosted by a weaker dollar and a recovery in global demand. \par \par \uc2  In good times and in bad, China walks all over its Southeast Asian neighbours in terms of exports thanks to its low labour costs. \par \par \uc2  But now the sliding U.S. dollar will deepen the pain for countries like Thailand, Indonesia and the Philippines, which compete with labour-intensive Chinese exports like shoes, t-shirts and toys. \par \par \uc2  China - with its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  virtually pegged to the U.S. dollar - will ride on the coat-tails of dollar weakness, its products looking even cheaper on shop shelves in Europe and Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Across the product spectrum and markets, China clearly leads the great Asian export revival story," Goldman Sachs economist Fred Hu said in a recent research note. "We regard China as a solid, and so far under-appreciated, growth story." \par \par \uc2  For its neighbours, these are daunting words about a country that achieved seven percent growth last year without much help from exports. \par \par \uc2  By comparison, Thailand's economy grew a slim 1.8 percent last year, Indonesia's 3.3 percent and the Philippines 3.4 percent, thanks in some part to robust agriculture and services. \par \par \uc2  CHINA'S GAIN, SE ASIA'S PAIN \par \par \uc2  All three countries saw their exports contract last year, policymakers blaming the drop at least in part for the trio's poor economic performance. But they have said they hope for at least some growth in exports this year. \par \par \uc2  China's exports never dipped into negative territory during last year's U.S.-led recession. \par \par \uc2  And figures so far this year show China's goods are flying off the shelves, 12 percent more going overseas in January to April compared with the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  "We expect (China's) export growth to remain strong, reaching 10 to 15 percent this year and continuing the trend begun in the first four months of the year as the U.S. continues to recover," said Ma Jun, economist at Deutsche Bank in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  U.S. imports crawled up month-on-month in March, still shy of their level the previous year just before the country entered a year-long recession, but they were well off lows reached in the depths of 2001's recession. \par \par \uc2  Although fears remain the U.S. economy could slip back into recession, many say more recovery signs are likely to emerge. \par \par \uc2  It's just bad timing for Southeast Asia that the biggest buyer of its goods has a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to go shopping with. \par \par \uc2  For U.S. buyers, Chinese goods will be as cheap as ever while in Europe and Japan - China's next largest export destinations - they'll look even more of a bargain. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has lost about three percent against the euro and five percent against the yen this year as U.S. assets lose some of their charm for investors who worry the United States cannot sustain its growing current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  As the greenback falls, the baht , peso and rupiah have become some of the region's fastest-rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : the baht and peso are both up about three percent since January, while the rupiah has gained a stunning 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  Although strategists say these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  still look more competitive than they did before the Asian financial crisis of 1997/98, China's mounting exports present a new challenge. \par \par \uc2  "With the weakening U.S. dollar, China's nominal effective exchange rate will go down two percent over the next 12 months," said Ma of Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  NO LONGER A STAND-OUT \par \par \uc2  China keeps its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trapped in a hair-thin band between 8.2760 and 8.2800 to the dollar through \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  does threaten many countries in the region - it's expanding its market share in low cost, high labour-intensive goods and it's moving up the product chain," said Paul Schymyck, economist at IDEAglobal.com in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "And China has been one of the major beneficiaries of the dollar's fall against the yen." \par \par \uc2  But economists say a stronger China is not all bad news for the region this year. \par \par \uc2  "There are two forces at play," said Ma. "China's continued growth means it will import more from the region at the same time as it takes away more market share in terms of exports." \par \par \uc2  Countries like South Korea have little to fear, having opted to focus their economies more on domestic consumption than external demand. \par \par \uc2  And Japan, Taiwan and Singapore depend more on higher-tech, higher-priced exports, which means they don't meet competition from China head-on. Malaysia and Hong Kong, both of which have pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will also weather the storm better. \par \par \uc2  "While China is no longer the stand-out 'defensive' market of 2001, its capacity to maintain steady, broad-based and moderately rapid growth should capture market interest later this year," said Hu of Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020524dy5o00hbb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
