\par\par \b \uc2  China Watch: China-Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Swap Deal Hints At Future \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kenneth McCallum \par \uc2 995 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2002\par 10:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- Japan and China are about the last nations you\par would expect to fret about speculative attacks from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Japan holds around $400 billion in foreign reserves - no other country has ever had anywhere near as much \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firepower at its disposal. China has nearly $230 billion, the second largest war chest in the world. \par \par \uc2  For the sake of perspective, Taiwan is a distant third in terms of foreign reserves, with $124 billion, and when George Soros famously "broke the Bank of England" with his attack on the pound in September 1992, the BOE had roughly $45 billion in reserves. \par \par \uc2  So why did these two Asian economic titans sign a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap deal last month to jointly fight against potential attacks against their monetary units? \par \par \uc2  The answer could say a lot about how the two countries might manage financial markets together in the future, as China becomes an even more important trading nation and bigger economy as a result of its entry into the World Trade Organization late last year. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, which are used for short-term liquidity management, parties exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and agree to swap them back at a future date at an exchange rate determined by forward rates. With these standing arrangements between central banks, authorities can access foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without buying it in the spot market, which would only worsen the plight of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under attack. \par \par \uc2  Financial officials tell Dow Jones Newswires that far from being just a symbolic move, the inking of the swap agreement - which allows China to swap up to $3 billion worth of yen from Japan for yuan and vice versa - heralds much closer coordination between Beijing and Tokyo in supervising the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "This is a very significant step," for China and Japan, says a senior Japanese financial official. \par \par \uc2  In a larger sense, the move shows how the two nations are becoming more economically intertwined, despite a deep and mutual mistrust that lingers from Japan's occupation of much of the mainland before and during World War Two. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    More Convenient \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The swap arrangement could be a useful tool for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in years ahead,\par as the country widens the trading band of the yuan in line with\par increased international trade. That move might tempt speculators to test\par the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's resolve to keep its exchange rate within a\par suitable range, the Japanese official says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If it needs to ward off speculators, the People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could,\par of course, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a conventional way to support the yuan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But to do that, it would first have to go through the trouble of\par selling off foreign assets in its reserves to free up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell\par in the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With the swap, China can simply exchange yuan - of which it has no\par shortage - for yen, a process that is more convenient and, more\par important, fast; in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , timing is crucial.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The arrangement follows a series of swap agreements that were signed\par between southeast Asian countries and Japan, China and South Korea over\par the past couple of years, as part of the so-called Chiang Mai\par Initiative.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Those arrangements are in place to make foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  available to\par the smaller Asian economies if needed to prevent more financial crises\par like the ones that hit the region in 1997 and 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Turbulence Ahead?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China, too, could expect slight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market turbulence in years\par ahead as it widens its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's trading band - something Beijing is\par widely expected to do.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Pu Yonghao, senior economist at Nomura International, predicts the\par yuan's fluctuation band against the U.S. dollar could widen to up to 3%\par in three years' time. That band is now only 0.3%, and usually the yuan\par trades in an even narrower range due to active PBOC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par Friday, for instance, the yuan moved in a miniscule range of 8.2772 and\par 8.2774 to the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In addition, the International Monetary Fund advised China in its\par latest world economic outlook report that "a gradual move to a more\par flexible exchange rate regime would also be desirable."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Judging from recent Chinese officials' remarks, Beijing appears at\par least ready to consider such advice.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par More international trade could eventually cause China's trade surplus\par to narrow, and a weakening of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at that point, made possible\par by a wider \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading band, could help the country improve its\par trade position.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It was with such future developments in mind that Japan embarked, two\par years ago, on its negotiations with China over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap deal, says\par the senior financial official.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One stumbling block for Japan during the bilateral talks was that\par because the yuan isn't a freely convertible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Japan didn't want\par the two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be able to swapped on equal terms. Instead, Japan\par asked for some "conditions" to be met before China could execute a swap,\par says the official.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the financial official didn't say specifically what those\par terms were, it's clear that because of the deal Japan has gotten more\par access to information held by China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  than it had before\par the deal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For one thing, the BOJ would be able to ask about the PBOC's books,\par since as part of the swap arrangement the BOJ now has an account in the\par Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Being able to get more information from the PBOC was in itself\par valuable for Japan, says the financial official, since China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has never been known for being the most open of organizations.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Also, "maintaining the swap arrangement will force the Bank of Japan\par and the People's Bank of China to stay in touch" and communicate about\par market developments, resulting in more policy coordination, the official\par says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Kenneth McCallum, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218-3268;\par djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1186 4252 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020426dy4q000qc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
