\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China says rates at right level, economy sound. \b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2002\par 11:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIJING, April 18 (Reuters) - China, fresh after reporting stellar first quarter economic growth, said on Thursday its interest rates were at an appropriate level and it would maintain its stable yuan policy for the next few years. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Wu Xiaoling also said China, pressured by its recent entry to the World Trade Organisation and ever closer links to the global economy, would strive to improve coordination of its interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  And it would gradually move toward yuan convertibility on the capital account, she said. \par \par \uc2  "The current level of interest rates is appropriate, the economic situation is sound," Wu said in response to a question on whether there was a possibility for fresh yuan interest rate cuts within the year. \par \par \uc2  In February, China cut yuan interest rates for the first time in nearly three years to help spur economic growth. \par \par \uc2  China's economy grew a surprising 7.6 percent in the first quarter of 2002, bouncing back from its worst quarterly growth in two years and beating analysts expectations of about 7.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  China, which was Asia's star performer with annual economic growth of 7.3 percent in 2001, has set an annual growth target of seven percent this year. \par \par \uc2  But there are fears the uncertain global economy may crimp its exports and over nagging deflation, and officials have raised concerns over how long it can sustain its massive deficit spending to sustain the growth. \par \par \uc2  China has resorted to heavy spending on infrastructure since Asia's 1997-98 financial crisis and has invested billions in new ports, airports and roads to help create jobs for millions laid off by reforms of the ailing state sector. \par \par \uc2  Wu, however, remained optimistic about the outlook for 2002. \par \par \uc2  "We are hopeful that seven percent economic growth will be achieved," she said. \par \par \uc2  STABILITY STRESSED \par \par \uc2  Wu said China would take steps to make the tightly held \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible, and adjust its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate policy as links with the global economy grow stronger now that the country has joined the World Trade Organisation. \par \par \uc2  But in the near term, there was little pressure on the yuan. \par \par \uc2  "We will maintain the policy of keeping the yuan stable for the next few years. Meanwhile, we will improve the formulation mechanism for the yuan exchange rate to make it more flexible," Wu told an economic forum in Beijing. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan currently trades in a narrow range of about 8.2760 to 8.2800, kept in check by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Chinese officials have said they were considering a wider trade band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cope with shocks as China opens up its markets under commitments to the WTO, but they have given no timetable and few specifics. \par \par \uc2  China joined the global trade group in December. \par \par \uc2  "After entry to the WTO, the link between China's economy and the world economy will become closer. Therefore, we will have to better coordinate our exchange rate and interest rate policy with the opening of the economy," Wu said. \par \par \uc2  Convertibility on the capital account in China is considered a longer term goal. \par \par \uc2  "On condition that we maintain yuan convertibility on the current account, we will gradually push forward its convertibility on the capital account," Wu said but gave no timetable. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say full convertibility for the yuan, now open only on the current account, is about five years or more away. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020418dy4i00j6a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
