\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Zhu says China no threat, vows to keep yuan stable. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Ruwitch \par \uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2002\par 16:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  QIONGHAI, China, April 12 (Reuters) - Chinese Premier Zhu Rongji on Friday sought to ease fears that China's growth will come at the expense of its neighbours' well-being. \par \par \uc2  Zhu, addressing an all-Asian gathering of businessmen and politicians, including prime ministers of Japan, Thailand and South Korea, said China would keep its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable. \par \par \uc2  He also brushed off criticism that the country's often-rosy statistics were faulty and backed a government target of seven percent economic growth this year. \par \par \uc2  "The Chinese people love peace. China's development needs peace. But an economically developed China will pose no threat to any country or region," said Zhu. \par \par \uc2  Zhu said China's membership in the World Trade Organisation, which it joined last year, and growth presented them with vast opportunities. \par \par \uc2  "A new leap forward in China's reform, opening up and modernisation drive will not only bring enormous benefits to the Chinese people, but also deliver unlimited business opportunities," he said. \par \par \uc2  Observers have said China must tread a cautious path between going all out to manoeuvre its size into strong economic and trade ties and easing worries about its growing economic and political clout in the region. \par \par \uc2  The two-day Boao Forum on tropical Hainan Island was an opportunity for China to do that and to boost its prestige on the international stage by hosting the Davos-style conference. \par \par \uc2  YUAN STABILITY, USEFUL STATISTICS \par \par \uc2  In a question and answer session after his talk, Zhu said China's yuan policy would remain stable. \par \par \uc2  "We have continued to adhere to this policy and this policy will not change. At least in the near future we will not change this policy," Zhu said. \par \par \uc2  Weakness in the Japanese yen this year has fueled concern around the region that China might devalue the yuan, sparking a round of competitive devaluations by Southeast Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  China keeps its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading tightly around 8.28 yuan to the U.S. dollar through \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked how he could be confident in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to reach its growth target of seven percent this year with criticism that data were bloated, Zhu extolled the national statistic bureau. \par \par \uc2  "China has already had this statistical system for more than 50 years and for more than half a century we have been able to squeeze the water out of the statistic figures," Zhu said. \par \par \uc2  "There is only a little water left, I cannot guarantee that there is no water," he said. "But even if there is water in the figures it is in comparable terms so most people in the world don't have doubts about the growth rate in China being less than seven percent." \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong called for Asian countries to boost financial cooperation. \par \par \uc2  "The lesson we have learnt from the Asian financial crisis is that we, the countries in Asia, should strengthen financial cooperation and set up a framework for monetary cooperation within the region," Dai said. \par \par \uc2  "Otherwise, we will be subject to financial speculation." \par \par \uc2  In March, Japan and China signed a yen-yuan swap agreement as part of an Asian-wide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  safety net designed to boost regional cooperation and ward off future financial crises. \par \par \uc2  FISCAL REVENUES RISING \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, Zhu said China's fiscal revenue was increasing by 15-20 percent annually. \par \par \uc2  "If we did not have this kind of development, and we didn't have such a big increase in our fiscal revenues, Boao would still be a fishing village as it was a few years ago," he said. \par \par \uc2  Hainan is where, about one year ago, China held 24 U.S. flight crew members for 11 days after a collision between their spy plane and a Chinese fighter jet forced an emergency landing. \par \par \uc2  The island has been spruced up to host business and economic chiefs including economics minister Li Rongrong and telecoms regulatory chief Wu Jichuan, Japanese economics minister Heizo Takenaka, and Thailand's foreign, industry and commerce ministers. \par \par \uc2  Though the meeting is billed as non-governmental, some leaders have scheduled time for bilateral talks. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020412dy4c00uf3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
