\par\par \b \uc2  Zhu tells neighbours China's growth not a threat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Ruwitch \par \uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2002\par 11:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  QIONGHAI, China, April 12 (Reuters) - Chinese Premier Zhu Rongji pledged on Friday to keep the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable as he sought to ease fears that China's growth will come at the expense of its neighbours' well-being. \par \par \uc2  In a keynote address at an all-Asian gathering, Zhu told businessmen and politicians - including the prime ministers of Japan, Thailand and South Korea - that China's economic health was vital for them and he backed a government target of seven percent economic growth this year. \par \par \uc2  He also brushed off criticism that the country's often-rosy statistics were faulty. \par \par \uc2  "The Chinese people love peace. China's development needs peace. But an economically developed China will pose no threat to any country or region," said Zhu, introduced by former Philippine President Fidel Ramos as "the CEO of China's economic modernisation". \par \par \uc2  Zhu told the Boao Forum on tropical Hainan Island that China's membership of the World Trade Organisation, which it joined last year, and economic growth presented them with vast opportunities. \par \par \uc2  "A new leap forward in China's reform, opening up and modernisation drive will not only bring enormous benefits to the Chinese people, but also deliver unlimited business opportunities," he said. \par \par \uc2  China is trying to boost trade ties with Asian neighbours while trying to ease their worries about its looming economic and political clout in the region. \par \par \uc2  The two-day forum was an opportunity for China to do that and to boost its prestige on the international stage by hosting the Davos-style conference. \par \par \uc2  YUAN STABILITY, USEFUL STATISTICS \par \par \uc2  In a question-and-answer session after his talk, Zhu said China's yuan policy would remain stable. \par \par \uc2  "We have continued to adhere to this policy and this policy will not change. At least in the near future we will not change this policy," Zhu said. \par \par \uc2  Weakness in the Japanese yen this year has fuelled concern around the region that China might devalue the yuan, sparking a round of competitive devaluations by Southeast Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  China keeps its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading tightly around 8.28 yuan to the U.S. dollar through \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked how he could be confident in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to reach its growth target of seven percent this year despite criticism of state statistics, Zhu extolled China's national statistic bureau. \par \par \uc2  "China has already had this statistical system for more than 50 years and for more than half a century we have been able to squeeze the water out of the statistic figures," Zhu said. \par \par \uc2  "There is only a little water left, I cannot guarantee that there is no water," he said. "But even if there is water in the figures it is in comparable terms so most people in the world don't have doubts about the growth rate in China being less than seven percent." \par \par \uc2  FISCAL REVENUE RISING \par \par \uc2  Zhu said China's fiscal revenue was increasing by 15-20 percent annually. \par \par \uc2  "If we did not have this kind of development, and we didn't have such a big increase in our fiscal revenues, Boao would still be a fishing village as it was a few years ago," quipped Zhu. \par \par \uc2  Hainan is where, about a year ago, China held 24 U.S. flight crew members for 11 days after a collision between their spy plane and a Chinese fighter jet forced an emergency landing. \par \par \uc2  The island has been spruced up to host business and economic chiefs including China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong, economics minister Li Rongrong and telecoms regulatory chief Wu Jichuan, Japanese economics minister Heizo Takenaka, and Thailand's foreign, industry and commerce ministers. \par \par \uc2  Though the meeting is billed as non-governmental, some leaders have scheduled time for bilateral talks. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020412dy4c00k7w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
