\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-China's Zhu says yuan policy unchanged. \b0\par\par\uc2 285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2002\par 08:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  QIONGHAI, China, April 12 (Reuters) - Premier Zhu Rongji said on Friday that China had no plans to devalue the yuan or shift the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its virtual peg to the dollar to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether China was considering pegging the yuan to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than the U.S. dollar, Zhu reiterated China's stand against yuan devaluation during the Asian financial crisis that ravaged Southeast Asian countries in 1997-98. \par \par \uc2  "Out of consideration for the interests of the region, and its own needs, China insisted on not devaluing its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We have continued to adhere to this policy, and this policy will not change," Zhu told an economic forum in the southern China province of Hainan. \par \par \uc2  "At least in the near future, we will not change this policy," Zhu said. \par \par \uc2  Weakness in the Japanese yen this year has sparked concern around the region that China might devalue the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sparking a round of competitive devaluations by Southeast Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  China keeps its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading tightly around 8.28 yuan to the U.S. dollar through \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In March, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Dai Xianglong said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  was studying an IMF proposal to peg the yuan to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket of its major trade partners, but analysts said this was a long-term consideration and the yuan would remain stable for now. \par \par \uc2  China's top priority is to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as stable as it can during what promises to be a turbulent teething period in the World Trade Organisation, and ahead of a top Chinese leadership reshuffle late this year, experts say. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020412dy4c00co2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
