\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: China To Begin Euro-Yuan Trades In Local Mkt Apr 1 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kenneth McCallum \par \uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 09:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- China will launch trading of euros against the yuan in its tightly-controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on April 1, a Chinese government official said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, the organization that processes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deals in China, sent out notices to members of the local interbank market saying they should prepare to trade euros for yuan, said Shen Jun, an official at the trade system's market department. \par \par \uc2  The Shanghai-based organization is currently testing a new system to allow euro/yuan trading, Shen said. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now only traded against the U.S. dollar, the Hong Kong dollar and the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  And trading of those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tightly monitored by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in the market to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a narrow trading range against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  In addition, China only allows the yuan to be converted to settle current account transactions. \par \par \uc2  Those policies are aimed at preventing the sort of capital flight that damaged other Asian economies during the regional financial crisis in 1997 and 1998. \par \par \uc2  Banking sources said China's decision to make the euro a locally-tradable foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't come as a surprise because authorities had discussed such a move for a long time. Local demand for euros is expected to gradually increase after China entered the World Trade Organization last December. \par \par \uc2  Recently, Beijing officials have also shown a willingness to deal more in the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to hedge against the government's holdings of U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Xiang Huaicheng recently said he plans to encourage the government to boost China's euro reserves. \par \par \uc2  People's Bank of China Governor Dai Xianglong said in January that euros ranked number two behind U.S. dollars in terms of total value held by China in its foreign reserves, which totaled $212.2 billion at the end of December. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Euro/Yuan Trades Not Expected To Be Active\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/yuan trading isn't expected to be as active as U.S. dollar/yuan\par trading, in which $100 million to $200 million worth is typically traded\par in a market session, banking sources said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There is some demand for euro, but not a lot, not significantly\par higher than (for the) yen," said a treasury official at Hongkong &\par Shanghai Banking Corp.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The HSBC official noted that at the moment, European companies are\par generally using the U.S. dollar as their main trading \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in China.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If people need to change yuan into euros now, they can do so by\par converting yuan into dollars first and then trading the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par for its European counterpart. But that costs more than simply exchanging\par yuan and euros, so companies that deal a lot in the European and Chinese\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be able to save on transaction costs starting April 1.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As with dollar/yuan trading, almost all euro/yuan transactions are\par expected to show up in orders by importers and exporters. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely give a guiding rate for the euro-yuan rate, the sources\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, which is only open for 90 minutes per\par session, showed no reaction to the euro-yuan decision. The dollar closed\par at CNY8.2767 Tuesday, marginally lower than CNY8.2769 Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Kenneth McCallum, Dow Jones Newswires, (86-21) 6218-3268;\par djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020312dy3c000h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
