\par\par \b \uc2  China To Start Euro-Yuan Trading April 1 - Official \b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 06:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- China will start trading the euro against the yuan in its national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from April 1, a Chinese government official said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, which is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in China, sent out notices to members of the local interbank market saying they should prepare for the launch of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Currently, the yuan is only traded against the U.S. dollar, the Hong Kong dollar and the Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading center is currently testing a system to allow euro-yuan trading, said Shen Jun, an official in the market department of the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading System. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in China is tightly monitored by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in the market to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a narrow trading band against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  In addition, China only allows the yuan to be converted to settle current account transactions. \par \par \uc2  Those policies are aimed at preventing the sort of capital flight that damaged other Asian economies during the regional financial crisis in 1997 and 1998. \par \par \uc2  China's decision to allow euro-yuan trading follows the government's move to increase the portion of euros in its foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  The China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading System, located in Shanghai, is where \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions are processed in China. \par \par \uc2  Euro-yuan trading isn't expected to be as active as U.S. dollar-yuan trading, in which $100 million to $200 million worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is typically traded in a session, banking sources said. \par \par \uc2  "There is some demand for euros, but not a lot, not significantly higher than (for the) yen," said a treasury official at the Hongkong & Shanghai Banking Corp. \par \par \uc2  The official noted that at present European companies are generally using the U.S. dollar as their main trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in China. \par \par \uc2  As with dollar-yuan trading, almost all euro-yuan transactions are expected to be to fill orders by importers and exporters, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to give a guiding rate for the euro-yuan rate, the sources said. \par \par \uc2  -By Kenneth McCallum, Dow Jones Newswires, (86-21) 6218-3268; djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020312dy3c0008s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
