\par\par \b \uc2  China Yuan Ends Higher On Steady US$ Sales By Exporters \b0\par\par\uc2 196 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2002\par 04:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- China's yuan closed marginally higher against the U.S. dollar Thursday on steady dollar sales by banks on behalf of their exporter-clients. \par \par \uc2  The yuan closed at CNY8.2765 as against Wednesday's CNY8.2766. It fluctuated between CNY8.2763 and CNY8.2767, but traders said most of the deals were done at CNY8.2765. \par \par \uc2  A Beijing-based Industrial and Commercial Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said he didn't detect any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even after the yuan hit its intraday high of CNY8.2763 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  China's yuan can only be converted for foreign-trade purposes, and most exporters must repatriate 85% of their overseas receipts to state-owned banks. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trading outside its former implicit band of CNY8.2770 and CNY8.2800 since last year due to the country's healthy trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was unchanged at CNY1.0607, while the Japanese yen edged up to CNY6.1767 per 100 yen from CNY6.1513 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By China Bureau, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218-3268; djnews.shanghai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1153 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020228dy2s000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
