\par\par \b \uc2  WTO Entry Should Make Chinese Yuan Even More Sensitive \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nicholas Hastings \par \uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2001\par 17:05\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- China's membership of the World Trade Organization will make the country even more sensitive about the level of the yuan. \par \par \uc2  But, there's no indication right now that the country would contemplate the devaluation that so many fear. \par \par \uc2  If anything, suggested Peter von Maydell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in London, it might be more appropriate for the yuan to be 'revalued' - rather than 'devalued' - given the recent strength of the Chinese economy. \par \par \uc2  "It is only just falling from overheated to sensible levels," von Maydell said, pointing to the high rate of industrial output and capacity utilization that is expected to ensure that the Chinese economy grows by as much as 7% this year. \par \par \uc2  During the third quarter this year, exports grew by about 3.9% from the year before. This was down from a rise of as much as 14% during the second quarter before global growth started slowing sharply. \par \par \uc2  China was formally accepted into the world trading body over the weekend in Doha, Qatar, where trade ministers from WTO members are discussing the launch of a new round of trade negotiations. \par \par \uc2  Mitul Kotecha, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Credit Agricole Indosuez, said concern about the competitiveness of the yuan will probably depend on how quickly export growth continues to slow. \par \par \uc2  Key data comes this Wednesday when trade figures for October will show just how far the events of Sept. 11 have affected global demand for its goods. September data, showing growth of 4.5% on the year, didn't reflect post-attack sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Certainly, China has been sensitive to keeping its competitive edge in the past. In March, when the Japanese yen fell back to as much as Y127 to the dollar, Chinese officials let their dissatisfaction be known. \par \par \uc2  Since then, the dollar has fallen back as far as JPY116 before rebounding to trade around current levels at JPY120. As Michael Lewis, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank put it, at Y120 the yen "is not at a level which is triggering contagion concerns." \par \par \uc2  At the same time, however, it must be remembered that the Japanese are keen to stop their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too far - with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  making it clear just this past weekend that they are ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needs be. \par \par \uc2  But apart from its exports, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has to worry about the Hong Kong dollar and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the U.S. dollar. The financial stability provided by Hong Kong is seen as vital to China's financial and state reforms. \par \par \uc2  "China wants to maintain the stability in USD/CNY exchange rate in order to prevent any negative impact on the Hong Kong dollar," asserted Marshall Gittler, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Achieving that though may not be so easy. Anticipation of WTO entry has already boosted foreign direct investment into China this year, helping to give the yuan some solid support. \par \par \uc2  But, if anything, Gittler reckoned, China would want to see the yuan weaker on a trade-weighted basis because it is betting that the dollar will actually decline. \par \par \uc2  In other words, he suggested, "China does not seem to be interested at all in allowing the yuan to appreciate after its WTO entry." \par \par \uc2  This still leaves the problem, though, of how long it might be before the country starts seeking a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure it keeps competitive edge. \par \par \uc2  -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011112dxbc005v7\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (china near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw)and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\